{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghrVg21DjraGSQmQb4ea","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MX7FnKHNaSaw5dfyQ5CGTV","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050223__x7910ab/work/PLAN.md","offset":1518,"limit":460},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12871,"cache_read_input_tokens":72009,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12871},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"4da2f063-9d1a-4040-b8ee-db0247fbd0fc","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:02:45.119Z","request_id":"req_011CghrVfmcVJUCFSHXtjcfW","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01MX7FnKHNaSaw5dfyQ5CGTV":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050223__x7910ab/work/PLAN.md","offset":1518,"limit":460}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MX7FnKHNaSaw5dfyQ5CGTV","type":"tool_result","content":"1518\t\n1519\t| Palette | Main colours |\n1520\t|---|---|\n1521\t| Spring | grass `#6abf4b` / `#9ad86a`, blossom `#f4b8d0`, sky `#a8d8f0`, water `#4a9ad8` |\n1522\t| Summer | gold `#d8b048` / `#e8d080`, red rock `#c84a2a`, haze sky `#f0d890`, savanna `#c8b060` |\n1523\t| Autumn | amber `#c87a2a` / `#a85a1e`, bark `#5a3a2a`, field `#d8a050`, sky `#e8b878` |\n1524\t| Winter | snow `#e8f0f8`, snow shadow `#8aa8c8`, ice `#a8e0f0`, pine `#2a5a4a`, sky `#b8c8e0` |\n1525\t| Hour | slate `#3a3a46`, brass `#d8c070`, ivory `#f0e8d8`, void `#14121c` |\n1526\t\n1527\tBattle ambient light by season: Spring .8 (cool green), Summer .95 (warm), Autumn .7 (amber), Winter .65 (blue). The\n1528\tHour places are dark (.45): brass lamps and the clock's glow light them.\n1529\t\n1530\tTASK.md treats the performance budget as unlimited, and FPS targets and optimization are out of scope. Add the detail,\n1531\tlights, shadows, weather and effects each scene needs, and spend no time on frame-rate work.\n1532\t\n1533\t### 7.2 The battlefield (`31-art-hexfield.js`)\n1534\t- **The floor** is a wall-less `E.TileMap({ w, h, floorTex, outside })` sized to the field plus 2 tiles of margin.\n1535\t  - `floorTex(x, y)` finds the hex with `G.hex.toCell` (cube rounding, not a search) and colours it with that terrain's\n1536\t    texture for the season and region. Use `E.tex.grass`, `dirt`, `water` and `flagstone` with palettes; write custom\n1537\t    functions for snow, ice, swamp, field, forest floor, ash and burning.\n1538\t  - Hex edges are darkened 18% within 0.8 units of the edge (the \"Grid lines: Bold\" setting), 8% (\"Subtle\") or not at\n1539\t    all (\"Off\").\n1540\t  - The margin uses the region's ground, fading darker.\n1541\t  - Re-bake with `map.floors = {}` when the season or terrain changes (fire, ash, the hub's bell seasons).\n1542\t- **Water:** tag the square cells under water hexes `'water'` (`map.setFloor`) so the engine's shimmer plays, and draw\n1543\t  2–3 moving dithered ripple arcs per water hex as decals.\n1544\t- **Props per terrain** (placed by a seeded rng per hex, within 8 units of its centre, recoloured per region and season):\n1545\t\n1546\t| Terrain | Props |\n1547\t|---|---|\n1548\t| forest | 2–3 trees: `tree` and `pine` props or store shapes (`kenney-nature` oaks, fall oaks and pines); blossom tint in Spring, fall oaks in Autumn, snowy pines in Winter |\n1549\t| hill | floor shading for a lighter top and darker rim, plus 1–2 rocks |\n1550\t| mountain | clusters of `rock` shapes (r 12–16); crystals in Hour places |\n1551\t| swamp | reeds (`grass` props, tinted) and puddle decals |\n1552\t| ruins | broken pillars and rubble |\n1553\t| field | crops (gold `grass` props), stubble specks in Autumn, snow in Winter |\n1554\t| bridge | a plank-bridge shape across the hex |\n1555\t| Keep | the `keep` shape (a round tower flying the owner's banner, with torch lights) |\n1556\t\n1557\t- **Raised blocks (`#`):** a shape at each hex centre, chosen by the map's `blockShape`:\n1558\t  - `hexblock`: `['cyl', mat, [0,0,0], 16.2, 24, { seg: 6 }]` (a hex prism), in the region's stone, with crenellations;\n1559\t  - `cottage`: Crossroads houses;\n1560\t  - `gear`: Meridian and the Sanctum;\n1561\t  - `cliff`: rock faces.\n1562\t  Units behind a block get the engine's x-ray silhouette.\n1563\t- **Decals each frame** (polygons through `r.w` of the hex corners at z 0):\n1564\t\n1565\t| Decal | Look |\n1566\t|---|---|\n1567\t| move range | blue `#3a9aff`, α .45 |\n1568\t| attack range | red `#ff3a4a`, α .4 |\n1569\t| area preview | gold `#ffd040`, α .6 |\n1570\t| path | white chevrons along the path |\n1571\t| Ward rings | a dashed outline of each knight's Ward in its side's colour; the active troop's also filled at α .12 |\n1572\t| danger overlay | a red hatch on hexes enemies can hit next bell |\n1573\t| cursor | a pulsing white-and-gold hex outline, plus a bobbing arrow over a unit there |\n1574\t| Sanctum | dials (lit or unlit) and the Hour Hand's glowing line |\n1575\t| fire | burning hexes: fire particles and a glow |\n1576\t\n1577\t- **Units:** `r.actor` with the rig, a shadow, and a side pennant above the head (the sigil on the side's banner shape).\n1578\t  A 12 × 2 px HP bar appears only for the unit under the cursor, the active troop and damaged units. Up to 3 status\n1579\t  icons sit above the pennant.\n1580\t- **Camera:**\n1581\t  - It eases to the cursor hex over .15 s; zoom ranges .7–2; turns are 60° steps with a .3 s tween.\n1582\t  - During AI turns it frames the actor, then the target.\n1583\t  - \"Battle camera: Dynamic\" zooms ×1.25 for spells and crits for a moment; \"Fixed\" never does.\n1584\t- **Views:** `board` (custom `new E.View('board', 'Board', 0, 50, 1)`) is the default, and `iso` is the alternate (V,\n1585\t  or the Settings \"Battle view\").\n1586\t- **Weather** (set in `enter`):\n1587\t  - Spring: rain .35 plus a custom `petals` kind added to `E.WEATHER`;\n1588\t  - Summer: clear, with faint dust (`sand` .1);\n1589\t  - Autumn: leaves .5 with wind .4;\n1590\t  - Winter: snow .6 (.9 for a moment during Blizzard).\n1591\t  Reduce motion halves every amount.\n1592\t- **Lighting:** canvas lighting with the season's ambient, Keep torches, a light per spell effect, lit dials and glowing\n1593\t  wisps (emissive). Call `game.enableGPU({ map })` where WebGPU exists (trees and towers as `lights.caster`), and match\n1594\t  `gpu.ambient` to the canvas ambient for the fallback.\n1595\t\n1596\t### 7.3 The world map (`32-art-worldmap.js`)\n1597\t- **A 64 × 64-tile `TileMap`** (1024 × 1024 units) in the `threequarter` view. `cam.free` pans within bounds; zoom\n1598\t  ranges .6–1.6.\n1599\t- **`floorTex`:**\n1600\t  - **Coast:** sea beyond a coast at radius 470 ± 30 of noise, by angle; deep blue water with shimmer tags and an\n1601\t    8-unit beach ring.\n1602\t  - **Land by sector:** north Winter, east Spring, south Summer, west Autumn; the ±12° wedges around the diagonals blend\n1603\t    two seasons.\n1604\t  - **Sub-terrain by noise:**\n1605\t    - Winter: snow plain, pine wood, ice lake, Rimewall rock;\n1606\t    - Spring: meadow, orchard, river, lake;\n1607\t    - Summer: savanna, red mesa, dune, oasis;\n1608\t    - Autumn: field strips, oak wood, bog, pumpkin field.\n1609\t  - **Hour plateau:** radius < 170, grey flagstone around Meridian.\n1610\t  - **Rivers:** 4 rivers (`E.Path`, catmull) from the plateau or the Rimewall to the sea.\n1611\t  - **Roads:** the 36 roads as gently wavy paths, 5 units wide, dirt or cobble.\n1612\t  - **Ownership:** a dithered tint of the owner's colour over each castle's Voronoi cell, re-baked when ownership\n1613\t    changes.\n1614\t  - **Season overlay:** snow dither in Winter (less in the south), blossom specks in Spring, warmth in Summer, leaf\n1615\t    specks in Autumn.\n1616\t- **Props:** trees per region (pines; blossoms; palms and cacti; fall oaks), Rimewall rocks and southern mesas,\n1617\t  watermills (`kenney-fantasy-town:watermill`), a few cottages, shrines, and Meridian's custom `clocktower` with a\n1618\t  working clock face.\n1619\t- **Castles:** custom shapes, about 40 × 40 units, in six styles that follow the region:\n1620\t  - `castle-winter`: grey stone, blue slate, icicles;\n1621\t  - `castle-spring`: white stone, green roofs, blossom;\n1622\t  - `castle-summer`: sandstone, red-and-gold domes;\n1623\t  - `castle-autumn`: timber and brown stone, amber roofs;\n1624\t  - `castle-hour`: black and white marble, a brass clock;\n1625\t  - `castle-brigand`: a palisade and tents.\n1626\t  The banner pole follows the owner and flies its sigil; on capture, the banner lowers and the new one rises (a tween).\n1627\t- **Troop figures:** one small Humanoid (size .7) per knight in front of its castle (up to 4, then a \"+N\" badge).\n1628\t  Questing knights show an \"away\" icon; wounded knights kneel.\n1629\t- **Markers:**\n1630\t  - the focus ring;\n1631\t  - a red \"!\" over castles a neighbour can attack this season;\n1632\t  - Stilled castles in grey with a stopped clock;\n1633\t  - realm-coloured march arrows (animated chevrons along the road, with a troop count).\n1634\t- **Resolution:** the marching figures walk the road (a 1.2 s tween along its `E.Path`), then a swirl transition into\n1635\t  the battle. The banner swap and a short fanfare or defeat sting follow.\n1636\t- **Season change:** the new season sweeps out from Meridian as a circular wipe with a dithered edge (1.5 s), while the\n1637\t  music crossfades and the weather changes.\n1638\t\n1639\t### 7.4 Story stages (`64-scene-event.js`)\n1640\t- Eight small sets in the `brawler` view, each a little TileMap with props and an `E.Backdrop`:\n1641\t\n1642\t| Set | Dressing |\n1643\t|---|---|\n1644\t| orchard (Avalorn) | blossom trees, the Queen's gravestone, petals |\n1645\t| sun hall (Brazenhold) | sandstone pillars, braziers, gold banners |\n1646\t| harvest hall (Ambergate) | timber walls, hay bales and pumpkins (`kenney-graveyard`), lanterns, a sundial |\n1647\t| ice hall (Rimeholt) | ice pillars (`crystal` props), blue light, light snow |\n1648\t| clock chamber (Meridian) | a giant clock face, gears, the Keeper's bier, brass lamps, darkness |\n1649\t| war tent | tents (`kenney-nature`), a map table, the speaker's banners |\n1650\t| road and field | a backdrop by region and season |\n1651\t| stable and roost | straw, fences, the monster in question |\n1652\t\n1653\t- The cast stands in a row. The speaker steps forward and faces the listener, with `pose` and `expr` per line. Emote\n1654\t  balloons come from the story module, and the camera tweens to frame each speaker.\n1655\t- `E.Dialog` sits at the bottom, with the speaker's portrait, the name in the realm's colour and the speaker's voice\n1656\t  (§9.3).\n1657\t\n1658\t### 7.5 Units (`33-art-units.js`, `engine-ext/beasts.js`)\n1659\t**Humanoids** (knights, and the humanoid monsters: treant, scarecrow, ghoul, golem, yeti, clockwork, stillwraith,\n1660\tskeleton, ogre, harpy, pixie):\n1661\t- Built with `new E.Humanoid(look)` from the data.\n1662\t- Swords and staves are native. Bows, whips, scythes, clubs and spears are drawn from `rig.hand()` and `rig.tip()`, as\n1663\t  the tactics kit's `bowOf` draws its bow.\n1664\t- Give each class line its own moves (the engine's rule: no two classes share a swing):\n1665\t\n1666\t| Who | Moves |\n1667\t|---|---|\n1668\t| Warrior line | Slash `slash`; Charge: run in, then `thrust`; Holy Strike `overhead` with a glow; Shield Wall `block`; Rally `cheer` |\n1669\t| Ranger line | Shoot `throw` with the bow drawn and an arrow on an arc; Volley rains arrows |\n1670\t| Mage line | Strike `overhead`; spells: a `cast` pose (.4 s wind-up with a rune circle at the feet), then the effect |\n1671\t| Cleric line | `rising` with the staff; heals use `cast` with a rising glow |\n1672\t| Beast line | Lash `backslash` with a whip line to the target; Goad `wave`; Roar `hips` with `expr: 'shout'` |\n1673\t| Rulers | Gareth `rising` into `slash`; Morgause `twohand` and `spin`; Pellam a slow `cast`; Mordrain a `spin` of the staff with ice; Meliant `cast` with clock effects |\n1674\t| The Gray Knight | `plunge`, `twohand` and `spin` at size 1.6, each with a .35 s wind-up |\n1675\t\n1676\t- **Reactions:**\n1677\t  - hurt: the rig's hurt pose and a flash; Guard: `guard` or `block`; victory: `cheer`;\n1678\t  - a fallen knight: `kneel`, then fades out under a white banner;\n1679\t  - a humanoid monster's death: `die`, then it dissolves into motes;\n1680\t  - Sleep: `down` .3 with z's; Stop: a grey tint and a clock icon; Chill: a blue tint; Burn: small flames.\n1681\t\n1682\t**`E.Beast` (new: `engine-ext/beasts.js`):**\n1683\t- **Constructor:** `new E.Beast({ kind: 'quad' | 'drake' | 'avian' | 'serpent', preset, size, colors: { body, belly,\n1684\t  dark, accent, eye, horn, wing, flame }, parts: { horn, mane, tusks, crest, fins, wings: 'feather' | 'bat' | 'none',\n1685\t  tail: 'long' | 'short' | 'snake' | 'flame' | 'pendulum' } })`.\n1686\t- **Skeleton** (3D, in world units from the feet, scaled by size):\n1687\t  - a spine chain of 4–6 nodes, a neck chain of 2–4, and a head;\n1688\t  - quad: 4 legs with 2-bone IK;\n1689\t  - drake: 2 or 4 legs plus wings (shoulder, elbow, wrist and finger tips for the membranes);\n1690\t  - avian: 2 legs, feathered wings and a tail fan;\n1691\t  - serpent: 12–20 segments that follow a path history at fixed spacing, with the head raised.\n1692\t- **Motion:**\n1693\t  - the gait comes from velocity (the phase advances with speed over stride; quads trot on diagonal pairs);\n1694\t  - planted feet use IK, the body bobs, the head counter-bobs, and a spring-damper chain sways the tail;\n1695\t  - fliers hover 8 units up and flap faster when moving;\n1696\t  - idle: breathing, ear and tail flicks.\n1697\t- **Actions** (`attack: { kind, phase, u }`, timed like `E.Attack`, with enemy wind-ups of .25–.35 s):\n1698\t\n1699\t| Action | Motion |\n1700\t|---|---|\n1701\t| bite | the neck reaches, the jaw opens, a lunge |\n1702\t| claw | a foreleg swipe |\n1703\t| gore | head down, then a charge |\n1704\t| breath | rear back, then thrust; it emits from `mouth()` |\n1705\t| tailsweep | the body twists and the tail sweeps an arc |\n1706\t| roar | head up, mouth open, a shake |\n1707\t| pounce | crouch, then a leaping arc |\n1708\t| dive | rise, then swoop down |\n1709\t| coil | the serpent wraps around its target |\n1710\t| stomp | rear up, then slam down |\n1711\t\n1712\t  Reactions: hurt recoil, sleep (lie down), and death (fall onto its side over .6 s, then dissolve into motes of its\n1713\t  element's colour).\n1714\t- **Drawing:**\n1715\t  - project each joint through the view (`view.p`, from the `r.actor` origin);\n1716\t  - sort the parts by camera depth (far limbs and wing first, then the body, then the near side);\n1717\t  - body segments are shaded ellipses (`px.ell` with `E.tones`), limbs are tapered strokes, wings are `px.poly`\n1718\t    membranes or feather stripes;\n1719\t  - finish with a 1 px dark outline, eye catch-lights, and horns, tusks, manes (flame polygons for Summer beasts) and\n1720\t    crests.\n1721\t- **Sizes on screen at zoom 1:** S 12–16 px tall, M 22–30, L 40–60 (dragons up to 70 with wings).\n1722\t- **`drawPortrait(g, x, y, size)`:** the head and shoulders, framed, rendered once per form to a cached offscreen\n1723\t  canvas.\n1724\t- **Presets:** `hound`, `wolf`, `horse`, `cat`, `boar`, `lizard`, `griffin` (eagle front, lion rear, wings),\n1725\t  `drake2` (wyvern), `drake4` (dragon), `avian`, `serpent`.\n1726\t- **`E.Wings` for Humanoids** (pixie insect wings, harpy feather wings): drawn behind or in front of the rig by its\n1727\t  facing against the view, flapping on a sine.\n1728\t- **Check them in the gallery** (`#gallery`, every species × form × action, cycling) and filmstrip each kind's walk and\n1729\t  attacks (§14).\n1730\t\n1731\t**Blobs** (`E.Blob`), with small overlays:\n1732\t- sproutling: two leaf polygons on top;\n1733\t- jack-o'-lamp: pumpkin ridges and an emissive carved face;\n1734\t- snowkit: ear tufts and frost glints;\n1735\t- minute: a clock face whose hands turn with time, and orbiting gear dots;\n1736\t- slime: translucency (α .8) and a highlight;\n1737\t- bat: wings and fangs;\n1738\t- tier 2 and 3 add a crown or aura sprite.\n1739\t\n1740\t**Portraits:** knights use `rig.drawPortrait`, beasts their `drawPortrait`, and blobs a scaled framed draw.\n1741\t\n1742\t### 7.6 Effects (`34-art-fx.js`)\n1743\t\n1744\t| Source | Effect |\n1745\t|---|---|\n1746\t| Spring | green and pink leaf and petal bursts (`particles` bits), vines growing from the ground (animated polylines), yellow pollen glints |\n1747\t| Summer | `particles.fire` and small explosions, a gold sun-disc flare (`r.glowDisc`), heat shimmer |\n1748\t| Autumn | spiralling orange, red and brown leaves; Harvest Moon: a pale-orange disc rises behind the target, then floods it with light; purple-orange wisp flames |\n1749\t| Winter | white-cyan ice shards (radiating polygons), cyan frost rings, snow bursts, a crystal that briefly encases the target |\n1750\t| Hour | a clock-face ring decal with sweeping hands, a grey desaturation flash (low-strength `game.flash`), brass gear bits |\n1751\t| Dawnlight and Turning | white-gold rays and glints, with a bell |\n1752\t\n1753\t- **Hits:** `game.hitFx` (hit-stop .05 s, shake, sparks, a bouncing number). Numbers are white, yellow \"CRIT!\", green\n1754\t  \"+n\" or grey \"MISS\". \"▲ STRONG\" or \"▼ WEAK\" floats over the target for .6 s.\n1755\t- **Statuses and buffs:** an icon each plus a tint. Poison shows green bubbles, Burn flames, Chill frost, Sleep z's and\n1756\t  Stop grey with a clock. Valor shows red chevrons, Haste speed lines and Warded a blue shield.\n1757\t- **Summoning:** a rotating rune ring in the element's colour (a decal), a pillar of light (emissive `r.queue`) and\n1758\t  rising particles. The monster scales up from 0 with a bounce, then roars.\n1759\t- **Capture:** a chain of light (a polyline of links) from the captor to the monster. The monster's pennant flips side\n1760\t  in a flash, then it walks to the captor and fades.\n1761\t- **Level up:** a gold column with stat gains popping up (\"+3 ATK\"), and a jingle.\n1762\t- **Promotion or class change:** a white flash and spiralling ribbons in the element's colours; the rig swaps to the new\n1763\t  form at the peak; a fanfare.\n1764\t- **The bell clock (HUD):** a 12-hour dial whose hand ticks to the current bell as the bell tolls.\n1765\t\n1766\t### 7.7 Animation checklist (each gets a filmstrip or gallery look, §14)\n1767\t- The 8 class moves and the 6 ruler signatures.\n1768\t- Every beast kind × its actions.\n1769\t- Blob idles and attacks.\n1770\t- Reactions: hurt, guard, sleep, stop, KO, retreat.\n1771\t- Summon, capture, level-up and promotion effects.\n1772\t- On the world map: the march walk, banner swaps and the season wipe.\n1773\t- The title lineup and the story poses.\n1774\t\n1775\t---\n1776\t\n1777\t## 8. UI and UX: 10-foot, controller first\n1778\t\n1779\t### 8.1 Rules for every screen\n1780\t- **Text:** body text is the engine's 5 × 7 font at a logical 320 × 240 (on a 1080p TV a line is about 32 px tall,\n1781\t  readable at 10 feet). Titles use scale 2–3. \"UI scale: Large\" doubles panel text, and layouts reflow to fewer rows.\n1782\t- **Density:**\n1783\t  - at most 8 rows per list (5 at Large), at most 6 cards per grid, and about 6 numbers per panel;\n1784\t  - stats are shown as bars with their numbers, never as stat tables (TASK.md: no dense web-style screens).\n1785\t- **Panels:** `E.ui.box` at α .85 or more over the world. Keep 8 px from the edges, clear of `game.cornerArea()` and of\n1786\t  the prompt bar.\n1787\t- **Focus:** something is always focused. The focused item gets a bright frame, an arrow and a small bounce, and moving\n1788\t  the focus blips.\n1789\t- **Every screen has a way back** (○, Esc or right click) and a prompt bar. A disabled choice says why in one line in\n1790\t  the footer.\n1791\t\n1792\t### 8.2 The input map (the game's own preset per scene; the engine keeps its menu keys)\n1793\t\n1794\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox | Keyboard | Mouse | Does |\n1795\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1796\t| `up down left right` | D-pad, left stick | D-pad, left stick | W A S D, arrow keys | hover moves the cursor and focus | move the cursor or focus |\n1797\t| `confirm` | ✕ (button 0) | A | Enter | left click | select, confirm |\n1798\t| `cancel` | ○ (1) | B | Esc, Backspace | right click | back, undo a move, close |\n1799\t| `command` | □ (2): tap opens the command menu, hold 0.6 s ends the turn or phase | X | Space (tap or hold) | — | the context menu: End troop turn, Delegate troop, Danger view (battle); Realm, Chronicle, Objectives, End phase (map) |\n1800\t| `info` | △ (3) | Y | I | middle click | details of what is focused; the backlog in story scenes |\n1801\t| `prev`, `next` | L1, R1 (4, 5) | LB, RB | Shift+Tab, Tab | — | cycle units (battle), castles (map) or tabs (panels) |\n1802\t| `zoomOut`, `zoomIn` | L2, R2 (6, 7) | LT, RT | `-`, `=` | wheel | zoom |\n1803\t| `danger` | L3 (10) | LS | T | — | battle: toggle the enemy-threat overlay |\n1804\t| `turn` | R3 (11) | RS | Q, E (left, right) | — | battle: turn the camera 60° |\n1805\t| `pan` | right stick | right stick | (the camera follows the cursor) | middle drag, screen edge (setting) | pan the camera; moving the cursor re-centres it |\n1806\t| `pause` | Options (9) | Menu | P, and Esc when nothing is open | the corner button | the pause menu |\n1807\t| `help` | — | — | F1 | — | How to Play |\n1808\t\n1809\t- Esc first closes whatever is open (the scene's `back()`, or the cancel action). Only when nothing is open does it call\n1810\t  `game.pause()`. Bind `pause` to P and Pad9 only, so the engine's Controls screen has no conflict.\n1811\t- Create (Pad8) is never bound. In a hub session, Options is ignored while Create is held (§11.6).\n1812\t- Menus repeat a held direction after .25 s, then every .08 s. The battle cursor steps to the hex neighbour whose\n1813\t  on-screen direction is nearest the input's (`G.hex` neighbour vs. `view.screenDirToGround`), with a fixed tie-break.\n1814\t\n1815\t### 8.3 Prompts and glyphs\n1816\t- **`PromptBar`** (bottom centre) shows 3–5 context actions as a glyph plus a verb, for example\n1817\t  `✕ Select  ○ Back  △ Info  □ Commands  L1/R1 Unit`.\n1818\t- **The glyph family follows the last device used** (`input.lastDevice`):\n1819\t  - **gamepad:** PlayStation glyphs by default: ✕ blue, ○ red, □ pink, △ green, with rounded labels for L1, R1, L2,\n1820\t    R2, L3, R3 and Options. If Settings → \"Controller glyphs\" is Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow, and LB, RB,\n1821\t    LT, RT, LS, RS, Menu;\n1822\t  - **keyboard or mouse:** a keycap sprite with `input.label(action, 'keyboard')`, plus the mouse-button icon when a\n1823\t    mouse button is bound too (`Enter / [L]`).\n1824\t- **The setting:** at boot set `game.input.glyphs = 'playstation'`, and the setting switches it live. If the engine's\n1825\t  own Controller \"button symbols\" setting exists, drive that one instead of adding a duplicate (check\n1826\t  `settings.keys('Controller')`).\n1827\t- Glyphs are sprites in `36-art-icons.js` (the 5 × 7 font lacks ○ □ △).\n1828\t\n1829\t### 8.4 The focus model (`50-ui-kit.js`)\n1830\t- A `Panel` holds focusable items in a 2D layout. The d-pad and stick move the focus, a mouse hover sets it (only when\n1831\t  the pointer really moves), a click confirms the hovered item, a right click goes back and the wheel scrolls lists.\n1832\t- Every widget reads one input: the active seat's in hot-seat, or every player's when shared (§10.8).\n1833\t- In battle, a hovered hex shows a small tooltip card; on the map, a hovered castle shows its castle card.\n1834\t\n1835\t### 8.5 Screens (layouts at 427 × 240 logical, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`)\n1836\t1. **Title:** the logo at the top centre (\"BRIGANTIME\" in `E.font.title` at scale 3, gold gradient, extruded; \"THE\n1837\t   LEGEND OF FORSEASONS\" below), the season diorama behind it, and the menu low in the centre: Continue (when a save\n1838\t   exists), New Campaign, Skirmish, Load, How to Play, Settings, About & Credits. The version sits at the bottom left\n1839\t   (`E.versionLabel()`). After 10 s with no input, the attract mode starts.\n1840\t2. **Campaign setup**, in four steps:\n1841\t   1. players 1–4, each claiming a device (\"Press ✕ on a controller to be Player 2\") or sharing;\n1842\t   2. realm cards, four side by side (banner, ruler portrait, season icon, a two-line pitch), one per human with no\n1843\t      repeats;\n1844\t   3. difficulty, with a line each;\n1845\t   4. Begin (the prologue plays).\n1846\t3. **Campaign HUD:**\n1847\t   - top left, the season wheel and \"SPRING · YEAR 1 · MUSTER\";\n1848\t   - top right, mana \"◆ 245 (+85)\" and the candle;\n1849\t   - at the bottom, the prompt bar;\n1850\t   - when a castle is focused, its castle card on the right.\n1851\t4. **Castle card:**\n1852\t   - name, owner sigil, season icon, mana, knights present (up to 6 rows: portrait, name, level, \"3/6\" troop, status\n1853\t     icon), the circle's species icons and the quest site;\n1854\t   - for your own castles in Muster, the actions: Organize, Summon, Move, Quest, Equip, Promote.\n1855\t5. **Organize:**\n1856\t   - three columns: knights (3 visible), the selected troop (a 2 × 3 grid of big monster tiles, the Bond pips \"Bond\n1857\t     7/9\" and the formation) and the reserve (4 visible);\n1858\t   - ✕ binds or unbinds, △ shows details, L1/R1 change knight, □ sets the formation.\n1859\t6. **Summon:** the circle in the centre, showing the focused species' live rig; on the left the species list (up to 6)\n1860\t   with costs; on the right a card with stat bars, element, size and upkeep. \"✕ Summon (−90 ◆)\" (or the reason it is\n1861\t   disabled).\n1862\t7. **Move:** pick a knight; the adjacent castles you own light up on the map; pick one, and the troop walks there.\n1863\t8. **Quest:** pick a knight, then a site card (a small rendered vignette, the name, two lines, the duration, outcome\n1864\t   icons and a risk level), then confirm.\n1865\t9. **Equip:** the knight's full-body rig on the left, the 3 slots, and an item list of 6 rows with stat changes (▲\n1866\t   green, ▼ red).\n1867\t10. **Promote:** the eligible units; a before → after preview (both rigs side by side, with bar changes); ✕ promotes.\n1868\t11. **Realm** (from the command menu): tabs Knights, Monsters, Items (a 4 × 4 grid with descriptions), Chronicle (the\n1869\t    event backlog) and Objectives (current goals and the candle).\n1870\t12. **March:**\n1871\t    - focus a target, then pick up to 3 troops (power bars and formations), then Declare;\n1872\t    - the target's card shows the defenders' relative power, the map's name and the season;\n1873\t    - an arrow appears on the map.\n1874\t13. **Resolution:** a card per battle (\"Solmark marches on Thornwick: 3 troops against 2\"), with Command or Delegate\n1875\t    when you are involved. AI-only battles show a quick result banner over the map.\n1876\t14. **Battle HUD:**\n1877\t    - top left, the bell clock \"BELL 3/12\" and the season badge with its Tide note;\n1878\t    - top centre, this bell's troop order as banners, the active one lit;\n1879\t    - bottom left, the active unit's card (portrait, name, level, HP and MP bars, element and size, statuses);\n1880\t    - bottom right, the targeted or hovered unit's card;\n1881\t    - on the right, the command list: Move, Attack, Special ▸, Spell ▸, Item ▸, Guard, Wait, and Retreat for knights.\n1882\t      Submenus are lists of up to 6;\n1883\t    - while targeting, the forecast (bottom centre, up to 3 targets, 1 line each): `FROSTWOLF  HIT 78%  23–28  ▲ STRONG\n1884\t      CRIT 6%`.\n1885\t15. **Results:**\n1886\t    - VICTORY or DEFEAT with the castle line;\n1887\t    - unit rows, 6 per page, with EXP bars filling and level-up gains popping up;\n1888\t    - captures and losses.\n1889\t16. **Tidings:** the season card (1.5 s, full screen), then report cards with icons (mana, quests, events). ✕ next; hold\n1890\t    ✕ to skip all.\n1891\t17. **Story scenes:** the stage, and the dialog box with portraits. ✕ advances, △ opens the backlog, and holding ○ skips\n1892\t    the scene (its effects still apply).\n1893\t18. **Pause** (the engine's): our items Resume, Save (Muster only), Load, How to Play and Dev Mode come before the\n1894\t    engine's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit to title.\n1895\t19. **Settings:** the engine's own, plus ours (§10.6).\n1896\t20. **Dev panel** (§10.3).\n1897\t21. **About & Credits** (§10.4).\n1898\t22. **How to Play** (§10.5).\n1899\t23. **The ending and the credits roll.**\n1900\t24. **Hot-seat card:** the ruler's large portrait, then \"QUEEN MORGAUSE'S MUSTER — pass the controller. ✕ Begin\".\n1901\t25. **Touch-only notice** (on the title): \"Controller or keyboard required\" with a large Settings button. The attract\n1902\t    mode still runs (§10.2).\n1903\t\n1904\t### 8.6 Accessibility\n1905\t- Sides carry sigils and shapes, and elements carry ▲ ▼; nothing is told by colour alone.\n1906\t- Status icons have distinct shapes.\n1907\t- The engine's comfort settings (shake, flashes, reduce motion) are honoured everywhere.\n1908\t- Text speed, hold-to-skip and UI scale are settings.\n1909\t- Single player has no timers; timers exist only in the hub and, if chosen, in hot-seat.\n1910\t\n1911\t---\n1912\t\n1913\t## 9. Audio (all code: no audio files)\n1914\t\n1915\t### 9.1 Music (`40-audio-music.js`, the engine's `{ bpm, steps, tracks }` format)\n1916\t**The Wheel motif** (`D4 - A4 - G4 - A4 - | F4 - E4 - D4 - - -`) appears in every main theme, changed by season.\n1917\t\n1918\t| id | Where | Key, bpm | Waves | Character |\n1919\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1920\t| title | title, setup | D Dorian, 92 | pulse lead (the motif), triangle bass, soft saw pad, light drums | heroic, wistful |\n1921\t| spring | the campaign in Spring | F Lydian, 104 | `pulse12` flute lead, triangle bass, hats | pastoral |\n1922\t| summer | the campaign in Summer | A Mixolydian, 116 | square brass, triangle bass, a snare march | proud |\n1923\t| autumn | the campaign in Autumn | E minor waltz (12-step bars), 96 | triangle lead, saw harmony, soft drum | wistful |\n1924\t| winter | the campaign in Winter | B minor, 72 | sine pads, high pulse bells, sparse | cold, still |\n1925\t| battle_attack | battles where the player attacks | D minor, 140 | square lead, saw bass, driving drums; a `tension` layer from bell 9 | urgent |\n1926\t| battle_defend | battles where the player defends | G minor, 128 | pulse lead, triangle bass, toms; `tension` layer | taut |\n1927\t| hourless | Hour castles, Dominion battles, the clock chamber | C minor, 100 | ticking `c` percussion, a falling chromatic saw line | eerie, mechanical |\n1928\t| boss | the Clockwork Sanctum | E Phrygian, 150, 13-step bars | square and saw leads, heavy drums | dread |\n1929\t| event | story scenes | the speaker realm's key, 84 | triangle and soft pulse | intimate |\n1930\t| ending | endings and credits | D major (the motif made major), 80 | everything | bittersweet |\n1931\t\n1932\t**Stings** (`audio.sting`, played once over the music): `season_spring`, `season_summer`, `season_autumn`,\n1933\t`season_winter` (2 bars each), plus `victory`, `defeat`, `levelup`, `promote`, `summon`, `capture` and `candle` (a low\n1934\tdrone with a tick).\n1935\t\n1936\t### 9.2 Sound effects (`41-audio-sfx.js`, with `audio.define` for the custom ones)\n1937\t- **UI:** `ui_move`, `ui_confirm`, `ui_cancel`, `ui_error`, `ui_open`.\n1938\t- **Battle:**\n1939\t  - `bell_toll` (its pitch steps per bell);\n1940\t  - weapons: `sword_clang`, `slash_whoosh`, `bow_twang`, `arrow_hit`;\n1941\t  - spells: `cast_charge`, `spell_spring` (chimes rising), `spell_summer` (a roaring whoosh), `spell_autumn` (a rustle\n1942\t    over a low tone), `spell_winter` (a glassy shimmer), `spell_hour` (a reversed tick-tock);\n1943\t  - support: `heal`, `buff`, `debuff`;\n1944\t  - statuses: `poison`, `burn`, `chill`, `sleep`, `stop`;\n1945\t  - outcomes: `crit`, `miss`, `ko_monster`, `ko_knight` (a low horn), `capture` (chains), `summon_rise`;\n1946\t  - creatures: `roar_small`, `roar_large`, `growl`, `chirp`, `flap`.\n1947\t- **World and timers:** `march_drums`, `banner_flip`, `castle_taken`, `candle_hiss`, `tide`, `timer_tick`; the engine's\n1948\t  `step` preset for footsteps.\n1949\t\n1950\t### 9.3 Voices: Animalese babble (`42-audio-voice.js`)\n1951\t- **Hook:** `E.Dialog`'s per-letter voice (engine change 1) calls `G.voice.letter(profile, ch)`. `G.voice.plan(profile,\n1952\t  text)` is the pure scheduling function (tests use it).\n1953\t- **Per character typed:**\n1954\t  - a vowel (a e i o u y) is a tone burst of 45–70 ms: `profile.wave` at `base × 2^(((hash(ch) % range) − range / 2) /\n1955\t    12)`, through a band-pass formant (a 800 Hz, e 1200, i 2200, o 600, u 400, × `profile.formant`), with a 5 ms\n1956\t    attack;\n1957\t  - a consonant is a 15–30 ms high-passed noise tick at low gain;\n1958\t  - capitals are a little louder; `?` bends the last 3 syllables up; `!` is louder and faster;\n1959\t  - `.` pauses 120 ms, `,` 80 ms and `...` 250 ms; markup is skipped.\n1960\t- **Profiles:** `{ base (Hz), range (semitones), wave, formant, rate, breath, vibrato, double }`:\n1961\t\n1962\t| Voice | base | range | wave | Notes |\n1963\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1964\t| Gareth | 220 | 7 | pulse | formant 1.1 |\n1965\t| Morgause | 180 | 6 | square | |\n1966\t| Pellam | 110 | 3 | triangle | slow |\n1967\t| Mordrain | 200 | 12 | saw | vibrato .03 |\n1968\t| Meliant | 130 | 4 | square | formant .9 |\n1969\t| The Gray Knight | 70 | 3 | saw | `double: -7` (a second voice 7 semitones down), slow |\n1970\t| Lunete | 300 | 6 | sine | formant 1.3 |\n1971\t| Tic / Tac | 420 / 360 | 5 | pulse | fast |\n1972\t\n1973\t  Everyone else is derived from the knight data's voice tag (for example \"lilting, high\" → base 260, range 9).\n1974\t- Monsters don't babble: they use the growl, chirp and roar sets.\n1975\t- All of it goes through `audio.sfxBus`, so it follows volume and mute. It is silent headless (no `ctx`) and when\n1976\t  Settings → \"Voices\" is Off.\n1977\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"823bd688-915b-45d7-962f-927d47066f69","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:02:45.266Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050223__x7910ab/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n| Palette | Main colours |\n|---|---|\n| Spring | grass `#6abf4b` / `#9ad86a`, blossom `#f4b8d0`, sky `#a8d8f0`, water `#4a9ad8` |\n| Summer | gold `#d8b048` / `#e8d080`, red rock `#c84a2a`, haze sky `#f0d890`, savanna `#c8b060` |\n| Autumn | amber `#c87a2a` / `#a85a1e`, bark `#5a3a2a`, field `#d8a050`, sky `#e8b878` |\n| Winter | snow `#e8f0f8`, snow shadow `#8aa8c8`, ice `#a8e0f0`, pine `#2a5a4a`, sky `#b8c8e0` |\n| Hour | slate `#3a3a46`, brass `#d8c070`, ivory `#f0e8d8`, void `#14121c` |\n\nBattle ambient light by season: Spring .8 (cool green), Summer .95 (warm), Autumn .7 (amber), Winter .65 (blue). The\nHour places are dark (.45): brass lamps and the clock's glow light them.\n\nTASK.md treats the performance budget as unlimited, and FPS targets and optimization are out of scope. Add the detail,\nlights, shadows, weather and effects each scene needs, and spend no time on frame-rate work.\n\n### 7.2 The battlefield (`31-art-hexfield.js`)\n- **The floor** is a wall-less `E.TileMap({ w, h, floorTex, outside })` sized to the field plus 2 tiles of margin.\n  - `floorTex(x, y)` finds the hex with `G.hex.toCell` (cube rounding, not a search) and colours it with that terrain's\n    texture for the season and region. Use `E.tex.grass`, `dirt`, `water` and `flagstone` with palettes; write custom\n    functions for snow, ice, swamp, field, forest floor, ash and burning.\n  - Hex edges are darkened 18% within 0.8 units of the edge (the \"Grid lines: Bold\" setting), 8% (\"Subtle\") or not at\n    all (\"Off\").\n  - The margin uses the region's ground, fading darker.\n  - Re-bake with `map.floors = {}` when the season or terrain changes (fire, ash, the hub's bell seasons).\n- **Water:** tag the square cells under water hexes `'water'` (`map.setFloor`) so the engine's shimmer plays, and draw\n  2–3 moving dithered ripple arcs per water hex as decals.\n- **Props per terrain** (placed by a seeded rng per hex, within 8 units of its centre, recoloured per region and season):\n\n| Terrain | Props |\n|---|---|\n| forest | 2–3 trees: `tree` and `pine` props or store shapes (`kenney-nature` oaks, fall oaks and pines); blossom tint in Spring, fall oaks in Autumn, snowy pines in Winter |\n| hill | floor shading for a lighter top and darker rim, plus 1–2 rocks |\n| mountain | clusters of `rock` shapes (r 12–16); crystals in Hour places |\n| swamp | reeds (`grass` props, tinted) and puddle decals |\n| ruins | broken pillars and rubble |\n| field | crops (gold `grass` props), stubble specks in Autumn, snow in Winter |\n| bridge | a plank-bridge shape across the hex |\n| Keep | the `keep` shape (a round tower flying the owner's banner, with torch lights) |\n\n- **Raised blocks (`#`):** a shape at each hex centre, chosen by the map's `blockShape`:\n  - `hexblock`: `['cyl', mat, [0,0,0], 16.2, 24, { seg: 6 }]` (a hex prism), in the region's stone, with crenellations;\n  - `cottage`: Crossroads houses;\n  - `gear`: Meridian and the Sanctum;\n  - `cliff`: rock faces.\n  Units behind a block get the engine's x-ray silhouette.\n- **Decals each frame** (polygons through `r.w` of the hex corners at z 0):\n\n| Decal | Look |\n|---|---|\n| move range | blue `#3a9aff`, α .45 |\n| attack range | red `#ff3a4a`, α .4 |\n| area preview | gold `#ffd040`, α .6 |\n| path | white chevrons along the path |\n| Ward rings | a dashed outline of each knight's Ward in its side's colour; the active troop's also filled at α .12 |\n| danger overlay | a red hatch on hexes enemies can hit next bell |\n| cursor | a pulsing white-and-gold hex outline, plus a bobbing arrow over a unit there |\n| Sanctum | dials (lit or unlit) and the Hour Hand's glowing line |\n| fire | burning hexes: fire particles and a glow |\n\n- **Units:** `r.actor` with the rig, a shadow, and a side pennant above the head (the sigil on the side's banner shape).\n  A 12 × 2 px HP bar appears only for the unit under the cursor, the active troop and damaged units. Up to 3 status\n  icons sit above the pennant.\n- **Camera:**\n  - It eases to the cursor hex over .15 s; zoom ranges .7–2; turns are 60° steps with a .3 s tween.\n  - During AI turns it frames the actor, then the target.\n  - \"Battle camera: Dynamic\" zooms ×1.25 for spells and crits for a moment; \"Fixed\" never does.\n- **Views:** `board` (custom `new E.View('board', 'Board', 0, 50, 1)`) is the default, and `iso` is the alternate (V,\n  or the Settings \"Battle view\").\n- **Weather** (set in `enter`):\n  - Spring: rain .35 plus a custom `petals` kind added to `E.WEATHER`;\n  - Summer: clear, with faint dust (`sand` .1);\n  - Autumn: leaves .5 with wind .4;\n  - Winter: snow .6 (.9 for a moment during Blizzard).\n  Reduce motion halves every amount.\n- **Lighting:** canvas lighting with the season's ambient, Keep torches, a light per spell effect, lit dials and glowing\n  wisps (emissive). Call `game.enableGPU({ map })` where WebGPU exists (trees and towers as `lights.caster`), and match\n  `gpu.ambient` to the canvas ambient for the fallback.\n\n### 7.3 The world map (`32-art-worldmap.js`)\n- **A 64 × 64-tile `TileMap`** (1024 × 1024 units) in the `threequarter` view. `cam.free` pans within bounds; zoom\n  ranges .6–1.6.\n- **`floorTex`:**\n  - **Coast:** sea beyond a coast at radius 470 ± 30 of noise, by angle; deep blue water with shimmer tags and an\n    8-unit beach ring.\n  - **Land by sector:** north Winter, east Spring, south Summer, west Autumn; the ±12° wedges around the diagonals blend\n    two seasons.\n  - **Sub-terrain by noise:**\n    - Winter: snow plain, pine wood, ice lake, Rimewall rock;\n    - Spring: meadow, orchard, river, lake;\n    - Summer: savanna, red mesa, dune, oasis;\n    - Autumn: field strips, oak wood, bog, pumpkin field.\n  - **Hour plateau:** radius < 170, grey flagstone around Meridian.\n  - **Rivers:** 4 rivers (`E.Path`, catmull) from the plateau or the Rimewall to the sea.\n  - **Roads:** the 36 roads as gently wavy paths, 5 units wide, dirt or cobble.\n  - **Ownership:** a dithered tint of the owner's colour over each castle's Voronoi cell, re-baked when ownership\n    changes.\n  - **Season overlay:** snow dither in Winter (less in the south), blossom specks in Spring, warmth in Summer, leaf\n    specks in Autumn.\n- **Props:** trees per region (pines; blossoms; palms and cacti; fall oaks), Rimewall rocks and southern mesas,\n  watermills (`kenney-fantasy-town:watermill`), a few cottages, shrines, and Meridian's custom `clocktower` with a\n  working clock face.\n- **Castles:** custom shapes, about 40 × 40 units, in six styles that follow the region:\n  - `castle-winter`: grey stone, blue slate, icicles;\n  - `castle-spring`: white stone, green roofs, blossom;\n  - `castle-summer`: sandstone, red-and-gold domes;\n  - `castle-autumn`: timber and brown stone, amber roofs;\n  - `castle-hour`: black and white marble, a brass clock;\n  - `castle-brigand`: a palisade and tents.\n  The banner pole follows the owner and flies its sigil; on capture, the banner lowers and the new one rises (a tween).\n- **Troop figures:** one small Humanoid (size .7) per knight in front of its castle (up to 4, then a \"+N\" badge).\n  Questing knights show an \"away\" icon; wounded knights kneel.\n- **Markers:**\n  - the focus ring;\n  - a red \"!\" over castles a neighbour can attack this season;\n  - Stilled castles in grey with a stopped clock;\n  - realm-coloured march arrows (animated chevrons along the road, with a troop count).\n- **Resolution:** the marching figures walk the road (a 1.2 s tween along its `E.Path`), then a swirl transition into\n  the battle. The banner swap and a short fanfare or defeat sting follow.\n- **Season change:** the new season sweeps out from Meridian as a circular wipe with a dithered edge (1.5 s), while the\n  music crossfades and the weather changes.\n\n### 7.4 Story stages (`64-scene-event.js`)\n- Eight small sets in the `brawler` view, each a little TileMap with props and an `E.Backdrop`:\n\n| Set | Dressing |\n|---|---|\n| orchard (Avalorn) | blossom trees, the Queen's gravestone, petals |\n| sun hall (Brazenhold) | sandstone pillars, braziers, gold banners |\n| harvest hall (Ambergate) | timber walls, hay bales and pumpkins (`kenney-graveyard`), lanterns, a sundial |\n| ice hall (Rimeholt) | ice pillars (`crystal` props), blue light, light snow |\n| clock chamber (Meridian) | a giant clock face, gears, the Keeper's bier, brass lamps, darkness |\n| war tent | tents (`kenney-nature`), a map table, the speaker's banners |\n| road and field | a backdrop by region and season |\n| stable and roost | straw, fences, the monster in question |\n\n- The cast stands in a row. The speaker steps forward and faces the listener, with `pose` and `expr` per line. Emote\n  balloons come from the story module, and the camera tweens to frame each speaker.\n- `E.Dialog` sits at the bottom, with the speaker's portrait, the name in the realm's colour and the speaker's voice\n  (§9.3).\n\n### 7.5 Units (`33-art-units.js`, `engine-ext/beasts.js`)\n**Humanoids** (knights, and the humanoid monsters: treant, scarecrow, ghoul, golem, yeti, clockwork, stillwraith,\nskeleton, ogre, harpy, pixie):\n- Built with `new E.Humanoid(look)` from the data.\n- Swords and staves are native. Bows, whips, scythes, clubs and spears are drawn from `rig.hand()` and `rig.tip()`, as\n  the tactics kit's `bowOf` draws its bow.\n- Give each class line its own moves (the engine's rule: no two classes share a swing):\n\n| Who | Moves |\n|---|---|\n| Warrior line | Slash `slash`; Charge: run in, then `thrust`; Holy Strike `overhead` with a glow; Shield Wall `block`; Rally `cheer` |\n| Ranger line | Shoot `throw` with the bow drawn and an arrow on an arc; Volley rains arrows |\n| Mage line | Strike `overhead`; spells: a `cast` pose (.4 s wind-up with a rune circle at the feet), then the effect |\n| Cleric line | `rising` with the staff; heals use `cast` with a rising glow |\n| Beast line | Lash `backslash` with a whip line to the target; Goad `wave`; Roar `hips` with `expr: 'shout'` |\n| Rulers | Gareth `rising` into `slash`; Morgause `twohand` and `spin`; Pellam a slow `cast`; Mordrain a `spin` of the staff with ice; Meliant `cast` with clock effects |\n| The Gray Knight | `plunge`, `twohand` and `spin` at size 1.6, each with a .35 s wind-up |\n\n- **Reactions:**\n  - hurt: the rig's hurt pose and a flash; Guard: `guard` or `block`; victory: `cheer`;\n  - a fallen knight: `kneel`, then fades out under a white banner;\n  - a humanoid monster's death: `die`, then it dissolves into motes;\n  - Sleep: `down` .3 with z's; Stop: a grey tint and a clock icon; Chill: a blue tint; Burn: small flames.\n\n**`E.Beast` (new: `engine-ext/beasts.js`):**\n- **Constructor:** `new E.Beast({ kind: 'quad' | 'drake' | 'avian' | 'serpent', preset, size, colors: { body, belly,\n  dark, accent, eye, horn, wing, flame }, parts: { horn, mane, tusks, crest, fins, wings: 'feather' | 'bat' | 'none',\n  tail: 'long' | 'short' | 'snake' | 'flame' | 'pendulum' } })`.\n- **Skeleton** (3D, in world units from the feet, scaled by size):\n  - a spine chain of 4–6 nodes, a neck chain of 2–4, and a head;\n  - quad: 4 legs with 2-bone IK;\n  - drake: 2 or 4 legs plus wings (shoulder, elbow, wrist and finger tips for the membranes);\n  - avian: 2 legs, feathered wings and a tail fan;\n  - serpent: 12–20 segments that follow a path history at fixed spacing, with the head raised.\n- **Motion:**\n  - the gait comes from velocity (the phase advances with speed over stride; quads trot on diagonal pairs);\n  - planted feet use IK, the body bobs, the head counter-bobs, and a spring-damper chain sways the tail;\n  - fliers hover 8 units up and flap faster when moving;\n  - idle: breathing, ear and tail flicks.\n- **Actions** (`attack: { kind, phase, u }`, timed like `E.Attack`, with enemy wind-ups of .25–.35 s):\n\n| Action | Motion |\n|---|---|\n| bite | the neck reaches, the jaw opens, a lunge |\n| claw | a foreleg swipe |\n| gore | head down, then a charge |\n| breath | rear back, then thrust; it emits from `mouth()` |\n| tailsweep | the body twists and the tail sweeps an arc |\n| roar | head up, mouth open, a shake |\n| pounce | crouch, then a leaping arc |\n| dive | rise, then swoop down |\n| coil | the serpent wraps around its target |\n| stomp | rear up, then slam down |\n\n  Reactions: hurt recoil, sleep (lie down), and death (fall onto its side over .6 s, then dissolve into motes of its\n  element's colour).\n- **Drawing:**\n  - project each joint through the view (`view.p`, from the `r.actor` origin);\n  - sort the parts by camera depth (far limbs and wing first, then the body, then the near side);\n  - body segments are shaded ellipses (`px.ell` with `E.tones`), limbs are tapered strokes, wings are `px.poly`\n    membranes or feather stripes;\n  - finish with a 1 px dark outline, eye catch-lights, and horns, tusks, manes (flame polygons for Summer beasts) and\n    crests.\n- **Sizes on screen at zoom 1:** S 12–16 px tall, M 22–30, L 40–60 (dragons up to 70 with wings).\n- **`drawPortrait(g, x, y, size)`:** the head and shoulders, framed, rendered once per form to a cached offscreen\n  canvas.\n- **Presets:** `hound`, `wolf`, `horse`, `cat`, `boar`, `lizard`, `griffin` (eagle front, lion rear, wings),\n  `drake2` (wyvern), `drake4` (dragon), `avian`, `serpent`.\n- **`E.Wings` for Humanoids** (pixie insect wings, harpy feather wings): drawn behind or in front of the rig by its\n  facing against the view, flapping on a sine.\n- **Check them in the gallery** (`#gallery`, every species × form × action, cycling) and filmstrip each kind's walk and\n  attacks (§14).\n\n**Blobs** (`E.Blob`), with small overlays:\n- sproutling: two leaf polygons on top;\n- jack-o'-lamp: pumpkin ridges and an emissive carved face;\n- snowkit: ear tufts and frost glints;\n- minute: a clock face whose hands turn with time, and orbiting gear dots;\n- slime: translucency (α .8) and a highlight;\n- bat: wings and fangs;\n- tier 2 and 3 add a crown or aura sprite.\n\n**Portraits:** knights use `rig.drawPortrait`, beasts their `drawPortrait`, and blobs a scaled framed draw.\n\n### 7.6 Effects (`34-art-fx.js`)\n\n| Source | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Spring | green and pink leaf and petal bursts (`particles` bits), vines growing from the ground (animated polylines), yellow pollen glints |\n| Summer | `particles.fire` and small explosions, a gold sun-disc flare (`r.glowDisc`), heat shimmer |\n| Autumn | spiralling orange, red and brown leaves; Harvest Moon: a pale-orange disc rises behind the target, then floods it with light; purple-orange wisp flames |\n| Winter | white-cyan ice shards (radiating polygons), cyan frost rings, snow bursts, a crystal that briefly encases the target |\n| Hour | a clock-face ring decal with sweeping hands, a grey desaturation flash (low-strength `game.flash`), brass gear bits |\n| Dawnlight and Turning | white-gold rays and glints, with a bell |\n\n- **Hits:** `game.hitFx` (hit-stop .05 s, shake, sparks, a bouncing number). Numbers are white, yellow \"CRIT!\", green\n  \"+n\" or grey \"MISS\". \"▲ STRONG\" or \"▼ WEAK\" floats over the target for .6 s.\n- **Statuses and buffs:** an icon each plus a tint. Poison shows green bubbles, Burn flames, Chill frost, Sleep z's and\n  Stop grey with a clock. Valor shows red chevrons, Haste speed lines and Warded a blue shield.\n- **Summoning:** a rotating rune ring in the element's colour (a decal), a pillar of light (emissive `r.queue`) and\n  rising particles. The monster scales up from 0 with a bounce, then roars.\n- **Capture:** a chain of light (a polyline of links) from the captor to the monster. The monster's pennant flips side\n  in a flash, then it walks to the captor and fades.\n- **Level up:** a gold column with stat gains popping up (\"+3 ATK\"), and a jingle.\n- **Promotion or class change:** a white flash and spiralling ribbons in the element's colours; the rig swaps to the new\n  form at the peak; a fanfare.\n- **The bell clock (HUD):** a 12-hour dial whose hand ticks to the current bell as the bell tolls.\n\n### 7.7 Animation checklist (each gets a filmstrip or gallery look, §14)\n- The 8 class moves and the 6 ruler signatures.\n- Every beast kind × its actions.\n- Blob idles and attacks.\n- Reactions: hurt, guard, sleep, stop, KO, retreat.\n- Summon, capture, level-up and promotion effects.\n- On the world map: the march walk, banner swaps and the season wipe.\n- The title lineup and the story poses.\n\n---\n\n## 8. UI and UX: 10-foot, controller first\n\n### 8.1 Rules for every screen\n- **Text:** body text is the engine's 5 × 7 font at a logical 320 × 240 (on a 1080p TV a line is about 32 px tall,\n  readable at 10 feet). Titles use scale 2–3. \"UI scale: Large\" doubles panel text, and layouts reflow to fewer rows.\n- **Density:**\n  - at most 8 rows per list (5 at Large), at most 6 cards per grid, and about 6 numbers per panel;\n  - stats are shown as bars with their numbers, never as stat tables (TASK.md: no dense web-style screens).\n- **Panels:** `E.ui.box` at α .85 or more over the world. Keep 8 px from the edges, clear of `game.cornerArea()` and of\n  the prompt bar.\n- **Focus:** something is always focused. The focused item gets a bright frame, an arrow and a small bounce, and moving\n  the focus blips.\n- **Every screen has a way back** (○, Esc or right click) and a prompt bar. A disabled choice says why in one line in\n  the footer.\n\n### 8.2 The input map (the game's own preset per scene; the engine keeps its menu keys)\n\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox | Keyboard | Mouse | Does |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `up down left right` | D-pad, left stick | D-pad, left stick | W A S D, arrow keys | hover moves the cursor and focus | move the cursor or focus |\n| `confirm` | ✕ (button 0) | A | Enter | left click | select, confirm |\n| `cancel` | ○ (1) | B | Esc, Backspace | right click | back, undo a move, close |\n| `command` | □ (2): tap opens the command menu, hold 0.6 s ends the turn or phase | X | Space (tap or hold) | — | the context menu: End troop turn, Delegate troop, Danger view (battle); Realm, Chronicle, Objectives, End phase (map) |\n| `info` | △ (3) | Y | I | middle click | details of what is focused; the backlog in story scenes |\n| `prev`, `next` | L1, R1 (4, 5) | LB, RB | Shift+Tab, Tab | — | cycle units (battle), castles (map) or tabs (panels) |\n| `zoomOut`, `zoomIn` | L2, R2 (6, 7) | LT, RT | `-`, `=` | wheel | zoom |\n| `danger` | L3 (10) | LS | T | — | battle: toggle the enemy-threat overlay |\n| `turn` | R3 (11) | RS | Q, E (left, right) | — | battle: turn the camera 60° |\n| `pan` | right stick | right stick | (the camera follows the cursor) | middle drag, screen edge (setting) | pan the camera; moving the cursor re-centres it |\n| `pause` | Options (9) | Menu | P, and Esc when nothing is open | the corner button | the pause menu |\n| `help` | — | — | F1 | — | How to Play |\n\n- Esc first closes whatever is open (the scene's `back()`, or the cancel action). Only when nothing is open does it call\n  `game.pause()`. Bind `pause` to P and Pad9 only, so the engine's Controls screen has no conflict.\n- Create (Pad8) is never bound. In a hub session, Options is ignored while Create is held (§11.6).\n- Menus repeat a held direction after .25 s, then every .08 s. The battle cursor steps to the hex neighbour whose\n  on-screen direction is nearest the input's (`G.hex` neighbour vs. `view.screenDirToGround`), with a fixed tie-break.\n\n### 8.3 Prompts and glyphs\n- **`PromptBar`** (bottom centre) shows 3–5 context actions as a glyph plus a verb, for example\n  `✕ Select  ○ Back  △ Info  □ Commands  L1/R1 Unit`.\n- **The glyph family follows the last device used** (`input.lastDevice`):\n  - **gamepad:** PlayStation glyphs by default: ✕ blue, ○ red, □ pink, △ green, with rounded labels for L1, R1, L2,\n    R2, L3, R3 and Options. If Settings → \"Controller glyphs\" is Xbox: A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow, and LB, RB,\n    LT, RT, LS, RS, Menu;\n  - **keyboard or mouse:** a keycap sprite with `input.label(action, 'keyboard')`, plus the mouse-button icon when a\n    mouse button is bound too (`Enter / [L]`).\n- **The setting:** at boot set `game.input.glyphs = 'playstation'`, and the setting switches it live. If the engine's\n  own Controller \"button symbols\" setting exists, drive that one instead of adding a duplicate (check\n  `settings.keys('Controller')`).\n- Glyphs are sprites in `36-art-icons.js` (the 5 × 7 font lacks ○ □ △).\n\n### 8.4 The focus model (`50-ui-kit.js`)\n- A `Panel` holds focusable items in a 2D layout. The d-pad and stick move the focus, a mouse hover sets it (only when\n  the pointer really moves), a click confirms the hovered item, a right click goes back and the wheel scrolls lists.\n- Every widget reads one input: the active seat's in hot-seat, or every player's when shared (§10.8).\n- In battle, a hovered hex shows a small tooltip card; on the map, a hovered castle shows its castle card.\n\n### 8.5 Screens (layouts at 427 × 240 logical, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`)\n1. **Title:** the logo at the top centre (\"BRIGANTIME\" in `E.font.title` at scale 3, gold gradient, extruded; \"THE\n   LEGEND OF FORSEASONS\" below), the season diorama behind it, and the menu low in the centre: Continue (when a save\n   exists), New Campaign, Skirmish, Load, How to Play, Settings, About & Credits. The version sits at the bottom left\n   (`E.versionLabel()`). After 10 s with no input, the attract mode starts.\n2. **Campaign setup**, in four steps:\n   1. players 1–4, each claiming a device (\"Press ✕ on a controller to be Player 2\") or sharing;\n   2. realm cards, four side by side (banner, ruler portrait, season icon, a two-line pitch), one per human with no\n      repeats;\n   3. difficulty, with a line each;\n   4. Begin (the prologue plays).\n3. **Campaign HUD:**\n   - top left, the season wheel and \"SPRING · YEAR 1 · MUSTER\";\n   - top right, mana \"◆ 245 (+85)\" and the candle;\n   - at the bottom, the prompt bar;\n   - when a castle is focused, its castle card on the right.\n4. **Castle card:**\n   - name, owner sigil, season icon, mana, knights present (up to 6 rows: portrait, name, level, \"3/6\" troop, status\n     icon), the circle's species icons and the quest site;\n   - for your own castles in Muster, the actions: Organize, Summon, Move, Quest, Equip, Promote.\n5. **Organize:**\n   - three columns: knights (3 visible), the selected troop (a 2 × 3 grid of big monster tiles, the Bond pips \"Bond\n     7/9\" and the formation) and the reserve (4 visible);\n   - ✕ binds or unbinds, △ shows details, L1/R1 change knight, □ sets the formation.\n6. **Summon:** the circle in the centre, showing the focused species' live rig; on the left the species list (up to 6)\n   with costs; on the right a card with stat bars, element, size and upkeep. \"✕ Summon (−90 ◆)\" (or the reason it is\n   disabled).\n7. **Move:** pick a knight; the adjacent castles you own light up on the map; pick one, and the troop walks there.\n8. **Quest:** pick a knight, then a site card (a small rendered vignette, the name, two lines, the duration, outcome\n   icons and a risk level), then confirm.\n9. **Equip:** the knight's full-body rig on the left, the 3 slots, and an item list of 6 rows with stat changes (▲\n   green, ▼ red).\n10. **Promote:** the eligible units; a before → after preview (both rigs side by side, with bar changes); ✕ promotes.\n11. **Realm** (from the command menu): tabs Knights, Monsters, Items (a 4 × 4 grid with descriptions), Chronicle (the\n    event backlog) and Objectives (current goals and the candle).\n12. **March:**\n    - focus a target, then pick up to 3 troops (power bars and formations), then Declare;\n    - the target's card shows the defenders' relative power, the map's name and the season;\n    - an arrow appears on the map.\n13. **Resolution:** a card per battle (\"Solmark marches on Thornwick: 3 troops against 2\"), with Command or Delegate\n    when you are involved. AI-only battles show a quick result banner over the map.\n14. **Battle HUD:**\n    - top left, the bell clock \"BELL 3/12\" and the season badge with its Tide note;\n    - top centre, this bell's troop order as banners, the active one lit;\n    - bottom left, the active unit's card (portrait, name, level, HP and MP bars, element and size, statuses);\n    - bottom right, the targeted or hovered unit's card;\n    - on the right, the command list: Move, Attack, Special ▸, Spell ▸, Item ▸, Guard, Wait, and Retreat for knights.\n      Submenus are lists of up to 6;\n    - while targeting, the forecast (bottom centre, up to 3 targets, 1 line each): `FROSTWOLF  HIT 78%  23–28  ▲ STRONG\n      CRIT 6%`.\n15. **Results:**\n    - VICTORY or DEFEAT with the castle line;\n    - unit rows, 6 per page, with EXP bars filling and level-up gains popping up;\n    - captures and losses.\n16. **Tidings:** the season card (1.5 s, full screen), then report cards with icons (mana, quests, events). ✕ next; hold\n    ✕ to skip all.\n17. **Story scenes:** the stage, and the dialog box with portraits. ✕ advances, △ opens the backlog, and holding ○ skips\n    the scene (its effects still apply).\n18. **Pause** (the engine's): our items Resume, Save (Muster only), Load, How to Play and Dev Mode come before the\n    engine's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit to title.\n19. **Settings:** the engine's own, plus ours (§10.6).\n20. **Dev panel** (§10.3).\n21. **About & Credits** (§10.4).\n22. **How to Play** (§10.5).\n23. **The ending and the credits roll.**\n24. **Hot-seat card:** the ruler's large portrait, then \"QUEEN MORGAUSE'S MUSTER — pass the controller. ✕ Begin\".\n25. **Touch-only notice** (on the title): \"Controller or keyboard required\" with a large Settings button. The attract\n    mode still runs (§10.2).\n\n### 8.6 Accessibility\n- Sides carry sigils and shapes, and elements carry ▲ ▼; nothing is told by colour alone.\n- Status icons have distinct shapes.\n- The engine's comfort settings (shake, flashes, reduce motion) are honoured everywhere.\n- Text speed, hold-to-skip and UI scale are settings.\n- Single player has no timers; timers exist only in the hub and, if chosen, in hot-seat.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Audio (all code: no audio files)\n\n### 9.1 Music (`40-audio-music.js`, the engine's `{ bpm, steps, tracks }` format)\n**The Wheel motif** (`D4 - A4 - G4 - A4 - | F4 - E4 - D4 - - -`) appears in every main theme, changed by season.\n\n| id | Where | Key, bpm | Waves | Character |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| title | title, setup | D Dorian, 92 | pulse lead (the motif), triangle bass, soft saw pad, light drums | heroic, wistful |\n| spring | the campaign in Spring | F Lydian, 104 | `pulse12` flute lead, triangle bass, hats | pastoral |\n| summer | the campaign in Summer | A Mixolydian, 116 | square brass, triangle bass, a snare march | proud |\n| autumn | the campaign in Autumn | E minor waltz (12-step bars), 96 | triangle lead, saw harmony, soft drum | wistful |\n| winter | the campaign in Winter | B minor, 72 | sine pads, high pulse bells, sparse | cold, still |\n| battle_attack | battles where the player attacks | D minor, 140 | square lead, saw bass, driving drums; a `tension` layer from bell 9 | urgent |\n| battle_defend | battles where the player defends | G minor, 128 | pulse lead, triangle bass, toms; `tension` layer | taut |\n| hourless | Hour castles, Dominion battles, the clock chamber | C minor, 100 | ticking `c` percussion, a falling chromatic saw line | eerie, mechanical |\n| boss | the Clockwork Sanctum | E Phrygian, 150, 13-step bars | square and saw leads, heavy drums | dread |\n| event | story scenes | the speaker realm's key, 84 | triangle and soft pulse | intimate |\n| ending | endings and credits | D major (the motif made major), 80 | everything | bittersweet |\n\n**Stings** (`audio.sting`, played once over the music): `season_spring`, `season_summer`, `season_autumn`,\n`season_winter` (2 bars each), plus `victory`, `defeat`, `levelup`, `promote`, `summon`, `capture` and `candle` (a low\ndrone with a tick).\n\n### 9.2 Sound effects (`41-audio-sfx.js`, with `audio.define` for the custom ones)\n- **UI:** `ui_move`, `ui_confirm`, `ui_cancel`, `ui_error`, `ui_open`.\n- **Battle:**\n  - `bell_toll` (its pitch steps per bell);\n  - weapons: `sword_clang`, `slash_whoosh`, `bow_twang`, `arrow_hit`;\n  - spells: `cast_charge`, `spell_spring` (chimes rising), `spell_summer` (a roaring whoosh), `spell_autumn` (a rustle\n    over a low tone), `spell_winter` (a glassy shimmer), `spell_hour` (a reversed tick-tock);\n  - support: `heal`, `buff`, `debuff`;\n  - statuses: `poison`, `burn`, `chill`, `sleep`, `stop`;\n  - outcomes: `crit`, `miss`, `ko_monster`, `ko_knight` (a low horn), `capture` (chains), `summon_rise`;\n  - creatures: `roar_small`, `roar_large`, `growl`, `chirp`, `flap`.\n- **World and timers:** `march_drums`, `banner_flip`, `castle_taken`, `candle_hiss`, `tide`, `timer_tick`; the engine's\n  `step` preset for footsteps.\n\n### 9.3 Voices: Animalese babble (`42-audio-voice.js`)\n- **Hook:** `E.Dialog`'s per-letter voice (engine change 1) calls `G.voice.letter(profile, ch)`. `G.voice.plan(profile,\n  text)` is the pure scheduling function (tests use it).\n- **Per character typed:**\n  - a vowel (a e i o u y) is a tone burst of 45–70 ms: `profile.wave` at `base × 2^(((hash(ch) % range) − range / 2) /\n    12)`, through a band-pass formant (a 800 Hz, e 1200, i 2200, o 600, u 400, × `profile.formant`), with a 5 ms\n    attack;\n  - a consonant is a 15–30 ms high-passed noise tick at low gain;\n  - capitals are a little louder; `?` bends the last 3 syllables up; `!` is louder and faster;\n  - `.` pauses 120 ms, `,` 80 ms and `...` 250 ms; markup is skipped.\n- **Profiles:** `{ base (Hz), range (semitones), wave, formant, rate, breath, vibrato, double }`:\n\n| Voice | base | range | wave | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Gareth | 220 | 7 | pulse | formant 1.1 |\n| Morgause | 180 | 6 | square | |\n| Pellam | 110 | 3 | triangle | slow |\n| Mordrain | 200 | 12 | saw | vibrato .03 |\n| Meliant | 130 | 4 | square | formant .9 |\n| The Gray Knight | 70 | 3 | saw | `double: -7` (a second voice 7 semitones down), slow |\n| Lunete | 300 | 6 | sine | formant 1.3 |\n| Tic / Tac | 420 / 360 | 5 | pulse | fast |\n\n  Everyone else is derived from the knight data's voice tag (for example \"lilting, high\" → base 260, range 9).\n- Monsters don't babble: they use the growl, chirp and roar sets.\n- All of it goes through `audio.sfxBus`, so it follows volume and mute. It is silent headless (no `ctx`) and when\n  Settings → \"Voices\" is Off.\n","numLines":460,"startLine":1518,"totalLines":2417}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01MX7FnKHNaSaw5dfyQ5CGTV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
