{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghxMRS96YjmtJDxmefuC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added a test suite, `tests/t-task.mjs`, that checks each TASK.md requirement directly in the running game, one test per item. It passes 15/15, and the full run, `node tests/run.mjs --browser`, now gives **93 passed, 0 failed** (10 suites plus the browser checks). The game is the single `BrigantimeTheLegendOfForseasons-BrigandineTheLegendOfForsena-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-050223.html` in this folder's root, and `exit-interview.json` beside it parses with every field filled.\n\n## What each TASK.md item was checked against\n| TASK.md item | What the new test checked, with its output |\n|---|---|\n| Single file, exact name | The one `.html` in the folder root matches the required name |\n| Built on the my-3D2dge handoff | The engine and the new creature-rig module are inlined in that file |\n| Audio all in code, gibberish voices | No audio files referenced; 11 songs, 11 stings, 41 sound effects; a line of Duke Mordrain's babble produced 13 syllables |\n| New art and animation | All 127 unit visuals (30 species × 3 forms, plus 37 knights) build and play an attack animation |\n| Playable from start to finish | A Verdance campaign on autoplay, through the real scenes, ended in a win in Year 2 (with the test-only stat boost) |\n| Dev mode with live sliders and reset | Reached from the pause menu: 29 sliders; mana income went 1 → 1.138 on the slider and back to 1 on Reset |\n| Attract mode | Started after 10.6 s idle on the title; Cancel returned to the title |\n| Multiplayer | Hub tags and client 1.4.0 are in the file; a 4-player hot-seat campaign builds |\n| Online play | The real AI Hub harness passed all four scenarios earlier in this session (2 players; 4 with latency and loss; host leaving; server mode); 9 hub protocol tests also pass |\n| Narrative tone | 61 story events in a formal court register (\"A clock, Your Grace…\") |\n| Controller-first prompts | ✕ on a PlayStation pad, A with the Xbox setting, Enter on keyboard, R-Click on mouse |\n| Mouse | A click on a menu row picks it. In Chromium, hovering moved the battle cursor across 10 hexes and clicking a unit opened its command menu. |\n| Touch-only devices | The notice and Settings button show, attract mode still starts, touch controls are off, and no scene has touch buttons |\n| All 13 game settings | Each one is read by the game code |\n| No TODOs or stubs | A scan of the 473 KB of shipped game code is clean, and the build refuses those markers |\n| About & Credits | Has the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback pages, and matches `exit-interview.json` |\n\n**Balance values:** changing them removed no feature. The Sanctum, the Gray Knight and the Season Dials are all in the game and the playthroughs win them. Only the numbers changed, because simulated Sanctum fights at the levels players actually reach were always lost, and TASK.md wins over the plan. The values stay adjustable with the dev sliders.\n\n**Testing counts:** the 21 issues are the specific bugs listed under Testing Feedback below. Each was found by a test or check run in this session and then fixed. The 6 critical ones are named there too.\n\n**Usage:** the API reports no cost or token counts to me, so those are unknown. Session time comes from the system clock, start to final check.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:23\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin / Atlus, PlayStation, 1998)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer.\n  - Campaign: three-quarter overhead world map.\n  - Battles: tilted overhead hex board (a custom 50° view, rotatable, with isometric as an alternative).\n  - Story: side-on stage scenes.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full build), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, Node 24 for the build and tests; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-style pixel look.\n  - Characters: the engine's character rigs and blob monsters, plus a new procedural beast rig.\n  - Terrain: hexes baked per season.\n  - Buildings: custom castle, keep, wall, gear and clock-tower shapes.\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal moves and poses, squash and stretch on blobs, new procedural beast animation (gaits, bites, breath, tail sweeps, dives, roars), plus particles, lights and weather.\n- **Audio:** all in code: step-sequencer songs built on a Wheel motif, stings, synthesized sound effects, and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode, \"Banners of the Wheel\" for 2–4 players. The host's copy runs the battle, the AI takes over a timed-out player's troop, and other copies resync from snapshots. Local hot-seat supports 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:** 10 headless Node suites behind one command, plus browser checks of the bundle and the AI Hub harness.\n- **Research:** none during the build; the plan records its own research (the Wikipedia article and an RPGFan review).\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time 1 h 14 min by the system clock (05:02:23 to 06:16:42); API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 21, all fixed:\n   1. The campaign AI never marched.\n   2. The Dominion snowballed across the map.\n   3. Autoplay never pushed toward Meridian.\n   4. The final battle could not be won at the levels players reach.\n   5. Experience came far too slowly.\n   6. Beaten brigand captains stayed on the map.\n   7. A duplicate top-level name broke the bundle.\n   8. A story event crashed when its flag was not set.\n   9. The tutorial battle came after the first Muster instead of before it.\n   10. Dev tools read stale state instead of the live scene.\n   11. The knight Owain joined at once in realms that start with a dragon.\n   12. Dev mode could not open while a battle dialog was showing.\n   13. In online play, the non-host copy sent no messages.\n   14. Debug shortcut links were dropped.\n   15. Characters missing from the pixel font showed as \"?\".\n   16. The final-battle shortcut put the wrong armies on the field.\n   17. The Summon prompt garbled its cost.\n   18. The Gray Knight looked like a red-haired man.\n   19. The camera chased the mouse.\n   20. A mistake in my own test scripts.\n   21. A second mistake in my own test scripts.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 6:\n   - the duplicate name (the game would not load);\n   - the AI never marching;\n   - the unwinnable final battle;\n   - the tutorial out of order;\n   - the silent online client;\n   - the camera chasing the mouse.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The autoplay campaign run through the real scenes, which became the playthrough suite.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Testing the dialog box outside the game loop. It could not see key presses, so it only showed that my test harness was wrong.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The deterministic headless clock and the debug handle, which make whole playthroughs testable in seconds. The character rigs, pause menu, settings and save slots came for free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - scene objects are wrapped, so the registered object is not the live scene;\n   - timers set before the first scene starts are cleared;\n   - the pixel font lacks common symbols, which show as \"?\";\n   - having the camera follow the cursor while the mouse hovers made the board slide.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hex battlefield (baked hex floor, picking, cursor steps by screen direction, hex-shaped overlays) and the beast rig.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting hex tactics onto a square-tile renderer.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** The dialog box now calls a voice function for each typed letter, so babble voices follow the text. I also added a new module, `engine-ext/beasts.js`, because the engine had no four-legged, winged or serpent bodies.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** First-class hex maps, plus a documented list of the font's characters with a visible fallback.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The precise deliverables and exit-interview shape, the freedom to change the engine, and the clear testing rules.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run (max effort, a different date and folder names) and assumed far more scope. The multiplayer instruction (\"as well as for the game on its own\") was ambiguous.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope, and tuning the campaign AI so the war actually moves and stays winnable.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark plan items as required or stretch, give a time or scope budget, and have the plan template take the run-specific names as variables."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":317,"cache_read_input_tokens":727402,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":317},"output_tokens":771,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"07026230-6ebe-4973-8253-6b277df78708","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:19:57.154Z","request_id":"req_011CghxMR5oaNL1eysp4S1HH"}
