{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghykwUvBziKMeT6Rw9Gm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I made one more balance change: the Hour-Candle (the countdown that loses the game when it burns out) now lasts 18 seasons on Easy, 15 on Normal and 12 on Hard, up from 16, 12 and 10. Most losses on Normal were the candle running out just before the player reached Meridian. The full run, `node tests/run.mjs --browser`, passes 99 of 99. The single `.html` is in the folder root, `exit-interview.json` parses with every field filled, and the About screen and exit interview show the same text.\n\n**Win rates without the test boost.** These are from the campaign simulator, with my autoplay planner playing the player's realm and every battle auto-resolved (never commanded by hand). 20 campaigns per difficulty, 5 seeds for each of the 4 realms:\n\n| Difficulty | Wins | Losses (candle burned out) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Easy | 12 | 8 |\n| Normal | 6 | 14 |\n| Hard | 0 | 20 |\n\nA human who commands battles should do better than auto-resolve, but I can't measure that: no test plays battles by hand. Four tests now each win an Easy campaign with no boost, one per realm. The boosted runs through the real scenes and the defeat path still pass.\n\n**Changes from the plan's numbers.** TASK.md says balance is key and wins over the plan where they differ, and it sets no values itself. The Sanctum, the Gray Knight, the Season Dials and the Hour-Candle are all in the game. What changed is their numbers:\n- Gray Knight stats are lower, and now scale with difficulty.\n- The candle lengths above.\n- EXP is doubled, and a new player drill gives a little EXP each season.\n- Dominion garrisons are one to two levels lower.\n\nAll of these are dev-mode sliders or data values. Every TASK.md requirement has its own test in `tests/t-task.mjs`.\n\n**Figures.** The 25 issues and 8 critical ones are the bugs listed below, each found and fixed in this session. Session time comes from the system clock; the API doesn't report cost or tokens to me, so those are unknown.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:23\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin / Atlus, PlayStation, 1998)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer.\n  - Campaign: three-quarter overhead world map.\n  - Battles: tilted overhead hex board (custom 50° view, rotatable, isometric alternative).\n  - Story scenes: side-on stages.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full build), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, Node 24 for the build and tests; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-style pixel look.\n  - The engine's character and blob rigs, plus a new procedural beast rig.\n  - Hex terrain baked per season.\n  - Custom castle and clock-tower shapes.\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal moves and poses, blob squash and stretch, new procedural beast animation, particles, lights and weather.\n- **Audio:** all in code: step-sequencer songs, stings, synthesized sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode, \"Banners of the Wheel\", for 2–4 players, with the host as authority. Local hot-seat for 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:** 10 headless Node suites and 7 browser checks behind one command, plus the AI Hub harness (4 of 4 scenarios passed).\n- **Research:** none during the build; the plan records its own research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time by the system clock, 1 h 35 min (05:02:23 to 06:37:48); API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 25, each fixed:\n   - The campaign AI never marched.\n   - The Dominion snowballed.\n   - Autoplay never pushed toward Meridian.\n   - The Sanctum couldn't be won.\n   - EXP was far too slow.\n   - Beaten brigand captains lingered.\n   - A duplicate top-level name broke the bundle.\n   - A story event crashed when its flag was unset.\n   - The tutorial was queued after the Muster.\n   - Dev tools read stale scene state.\n   - Owain joined too early.\n   - Dev mode was blocked during a battle dialog.\n   - The online client sent nothing.\n   - Deep links were dropped.\n   - Characters missing from the font showed as \"?\".\n   - The Sanctum deep link fielded the wrong sides.\n   - The Summon prompt mangled its cost.\n   - The Gray Knight looked like a red-haired man.\n   - The camera chased the mouse.\n   - Without the boost the player never won.\n   - The Dominion fought its own Gray Knight.\n   - The Sanctum was skipped when few knights survived.\n   - The Sanctum reinforcement rule was missing.\n   - Two mistakes in my own test scripts.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 8:\n   - the duplicate name (the game would not load);\n   - the AI never marching;\n   - the unwinnable Sanctum;\n   - no win without the boost;\n   - the Dominion fighting itself (a possible false loss);\n   - the tutorial out of order;\n   - the silent online client;\n   - the camera chasing the mouse.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The autoplay campaign, especially run without the boost.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Testing the dialog box outside the game loop.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The deterministic headless clock and the debug handle. The character rigs, pause menu, settings and saves come free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - Scenes are wrapped, so the registered object isn't the live scene.\n   - Timers set before the first scene are cleared.\n   - The font has gaps that show as \"?\".\n   - Camera-follow made the board slide under mouse hover.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hex battlefield and the beast rig.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting hex tactics onto a square-tile renderer.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** A per-letter voice callback in the dialog box, so babble follows the text, and a new module, `engine-ext/beasts.js`, for creature bodies the engine lacked.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** First-class hex maps, plus a documented glyph list for the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The precise deliverables, the freedom to change the engine, and the clear testing rules.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run and assumed far more scope. The multiplayer wording \"as well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope, and balancing the campaign so it can be won without help.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark plan items as required or stretch, give a scope budget, and have the plan take its run-specific names from TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":423,"cache_read_input_tokens":769589,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":423},"output_tokens":740,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"416ade88-f3dc-47bb-9ae3-8972d59af626","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:38:16.980Z","request_id":"req_011Cghykw5rPWiwdtR5NGg1X"}
