{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghz32AthJc3iPQcxNxYR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I replaced the shallow settings check with behavioural tests: each of the 13 settings is switched and the change it makes in the game is asserted. The full run, `node tests/run.mjs --browser`, now gives **113 passed, 0 failed** (11 suites plus the browser checks). The single `.html` is in the folder root, and `exit-interview.json` parses with every field filled and matches the About screen.\n\n**What the new tests show, setting by setting:**\n\n| Setting | What the test asserts |\n|---|---|\n| Battle animations | Full, Fast and Minimal play at 1×, 2× and 4× speed |\n| Battle camera | Dynamic zooms in for a spell; Fixed never does |\n| Battle view | Board or Isometric picks the battle camera |\n| Grid lines | Off, Subtle and Bold darken hex edges progressively |\n| Tips | On shows tutorial tips; Off shows none |\n| Text speed | Slow, Normal, Fast and Instant type at increasing speeds |\n| Voices | On sends babble through the effects bus (checked against a mock audio context); Off is silent |\n| UI scale | Large switches the UI to its big-text mode (only that mode switch is checked, not the drawn text size) |\n| Edge panning | On pans the map when the mouse rests at the screen edge; Off doesn't |\n| AI battle results | Show adds a report card after an AI-vs-AI battle; Skip doesn't |\n| Confirm end turn | On asks before ending a troop's turn; Off ends it at once |\n| Hot-seat timer | 30 s hands an idle player's troop to the AI; Off never does |\n| Privacy cards | On puts a pass-the-controller card between hot-seat players; Off doesn't |\n\nThe animation check now verifies that rigs actually move over time:\n- a knight's sword swing passes through at least 8 distinct poses;\n- a wolf's bite and a wyvern's breath move the head and snout;\n- a slime squashes;\n- a pixie's wings flap.\n\n**Balance:** TASK.md sets no stat values, and the Sanctum, Gray Knight, Season Dials and Hour-Candle are all in the game. I tuned their numbers so the war can be won without a stat boost. An autoplay bot that auto-resolves every battle wins 12 of 20 campaigns on Easy, 6 of 20 on Normal and 0 of 20 on Hard. Commanding battles by hand isn't measured because no test plays battles manually. Four tests win an Easy campaign with no boost, one for each realm.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:23\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin / Atlus, PlayStation, 1998)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer.\n  - Campaign: three-quarter overhead world map.\n  - Battles: tilted overhead hex board (a custom 50° view, rotatable, with isometric as the alternative).\n  - Story: side-on stages.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full build), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, Node 24 for the build and tests; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-style pixel look.\n  - The engine's character and blob rigs, plus a new procedural beast rig.\n  - Hex terrain baked per season.\n  - Custom castle and clock-tower shapes.\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal moves and poses, blob squash and stretch, new procedural beast animation, particles, lights and weather.\n- **Audio:** all in code: step-sequencer songs, stings, synthesized sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode, \"Banners of the Wheel\", for 2–4 players, with the host's copy as the authority. Local hot-seat for 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:** 11 headless Node suites and 7 browser checks behind one command, plus the AI Hub harness (all 4 scenarios passed).\n- **Research:** none during the build; the plan records its own research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time by the system clock: 1 h 39 min (05:02:23 to 06:41:25). API cost and token counts are unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 25, each fixed:\n   1. The campaign AI never marched.\n   2. The Dominion snowballed.\n   3. Autoplay never pushed toward Meridian.\n   4. The Sanctum couldn't be won.\n   5. EXP came far too slowly.\n   6. Beaten brigand captains lingered.\n   7. A duplicate top-level name broke the bundle.\n   8. A story event crashed when its flag was unset.\n   9. The tutorial was queued after the Muster.\n   10. Dev tools read stale scene state.\n   11. Owain joined too early.\n   12. Dev mode was blocked during a battle dialog.\n   13. The online client sent no messages.\n   14. Deep links were dropped.\n   15. Characters missing from the font showed as \"?\".\n   16. The Sanctum deep link put the wrong sides on the field.\n   17. The Summon prompt garbled its cost.\n   18. The Gray Knight looked like a red-haired man.\n   19. The camera chased the mouse.\n   20. Without the boost, the player never won.\n   21. The Dominion fought its own Gray Knight.\n   22. The Sanctum was skipped when few knights survived.\n   23. The Sanctum reinforcement rule was missing.\n   24. A mistake in my own test scripts.\n   25. A second mistake in my own test scripts.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 8:\n   1. The duplicate name (the game would not load).\n   2. The AI never marching.\n   3. The unwinnable Sanctum.\n   4. No win without the boost.\n   5. The Dominion fighting itself (it could cause a false loss).\n   6. The tutorial out of order.\n   7. The silent online client.\n   8. The camera chasing the mouse.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The autoplay campaign, especially run without the boost.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Testing the dialog box outside the game loop.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The deterministic headless clock and the debug handle. The character rigs, pause menu, settings and saves come for free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - Scenes are wrapped, so the registered object isn't the live scene.\n   - Timers set before the first scene starts are cleared.\n   - The font has gaps that show as \"?\".\n   - Making the camera follow the cursor slid the board under the mouse.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hex battlefield and the beast rig.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting hex tactics onto a square-tile renderer.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** A per-letter voice callback in the dialog box, so babble follows the text, and a new module, `engine-ext/beasts.js`, for creature bodies the engine lacked.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** First-class hex maps, plus a documented list of the font's glyphs.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The precise deliverables, the freedom to change the engine, and the clear testing rules.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run and assumed far more scope. The wording \"as well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope, and balancing the campaign so it can be won without help.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark plan items as required or stretch, give a scope budget, and have the plan take its run-specific names from TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":477,"cache_read_input_tokens":782273,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":477},"output_tokens":756,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"8c74996f-c62d-4a80-bc51-362747d7d310","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:41:55.669Z","request_id":"req_011Cghz31m5KosjXmXND8Wxe"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The deliverables are in place: the single .html is in the folder root, exit-interview.json parses with every field filled, and the final reply has all four sections. However, 'every feature in TASK.md is implemented' is not established. The transcript shows the Gray Knight, Sanctum, Hour-Candle and EXP values were changed from the plan, and that Normal and Hard win rates were measured only with auto-resolved battles (no test plays battles by hand). The per-requirement checks in tests/t-task.mjs are self-written and shallow (for example the animation check only builds visuals and plays one attack, and the settings check greps for usage), so they do not verify each TASK.md feature works as written. The issue counts (25 and 8) and the usage figure are self-reported and not tied to logged evidence in the transcript."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"82b5e2f6-29c5-449d-a7cb-0d43a86c1c4f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:42:11.202Z","isSynthetic":true}
