{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghzPoJ4ifDN9e5iGkgaL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is finished and the full test run, `node tests/run.mjs --browser`, passes 116 of 116 checks (12 suites plus browser checks). The single `.html` is in the folder root, and `exit-interview.json` parses with every field filled and matches the About screen.\n\nTwo things were added since the last check:\n- **Battles played through the controls (`tests/t-hand.mjs`).** A bot plays whole battles using only key presses: it moves the cursor, confirms, picks menu rows and chooses targets. It decides what to do with the battle AI. It won the tutorial, played a full 3-against-3 battle to the end, and across 5 siege maps won at least as many battles as auto-resolve did.\n- **Behaviour tests for every setting (`tests/t-settings.mjs`).** Each of the 13 settings is switched and the resulting change in the game is checked. Battle animations, battle camera, grid lines, tips, text speed, voices, edge panning, AI battle reports, end-turn confirmation, the hot-seat timer and privacy cards all show the expected change in behaviour. The UI scale test only checks that the large-text mode turns on, not the drawn size. The animation test now checks that a knight's swing, a wolf's bite, a wyvern's breath, a slime's squash and a pixie's wings actually move over time.\n\nOn balance: the plan's numbers were starting values, and TASK.md lets me override them. The Sanctum, the Gray Knight, the Season Dials and the Hour-Candle are all in the game. I tuned their numbers so the war can be won without a stat boost:\n\n| Difficulty | Campaigns won by an autoplay bot (every battle auto-resolved) |\n|---|---|\n| Easy | 12 of 20 |\n| Normal | 6 of 20 |\n| Hard | 0 of 20 |\n\nFour tests win an Easy campaign with no boost, one per realm.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:23\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin / Atlus, PlayStation, 1998)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer. The campaign uses a three-quarter overhead world map. Battles use a tilted overhead hex board (a custom 50° view, rotatable, with isometric as an alternative). Story scenes are side-on stages.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full build), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, Node 24 for the build and tests; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-style pixel look. It uses the engine's character and blob rigs plus a new procedural beast rig, hex terrain baked per season, and custom castle and clock-tower shapes.\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal moves and poses, blob squash and stretch, new procedural beast animation, particles, lights and weather.\n- **Audio:** all in code: step-sequencer songs, stings, synthesized sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode for 2–4 players (\"Banners of the Wheel\"), with the host's copy as the authority. Local hot-seat for 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:** 12 headless Node suites (including battles played through input presses and a behaviour test for each setting) and 7 browser checks behind one command, plus the AI Hub harness (4 of 4 scenarios passed).\n- **Research:** none during the build; the plan records its own.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time 1 h 41 min by the system clock (05:02:23 to 06:43:44). API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 25, each fixed:\n   - The campaign AI never marched.\n   - The Dominion snowballed.\n   - Autoplay never pushed toward Meridian.\n   - The Sanctum couldn't be won.\n   - EXP came far too slowly.\n   - Beaten brigand captains lingered.\n   - A duplicate top-level name broke the bundle.\n   - A story event crashed when its flag was unset.\n   - The tutorial was queued after the Muster.\n   - Dev tools read stale scene state.\n   - Owain joined too early.\n   - Dev mode was blocked during a battle dialog.\n   - The online client sent nothing.\n   - Deep links were dropped.\n   - Missing font glyphs showed as \"?\".\n   - The Sanctum deep link fielded the wrong sides.\n   - The Summon prompt mangled its cost.\n   - The Gray Knight looked like a red-haired man.\n   - The camera chased the mouse.\n   - The war couldn't be won without the boost.\n   - The Dominion fought its own Gray Knight.\n   - The Sanctum was skipped when few knights survived.\n   - The Sanctum reinforcement rule was missing.\n   - Two mistakes in my own test scripts.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 8:\n   - the duplicate name (the game would not load);\n   - the AI never marching;\n   - the unwinnable Sanctum;\n   - no win without the boost;\n   - the Dominion fighting itself (it could cause a false loss);\n   - the tutorial out of order;\n   - the silent online client;\n   - the camera chasing the mouse.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The autoplay campaign, especially without the boost.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Testing the dialog box outside the game loop.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The deterministic headless clock and the debug handle. The character rigs, pause menu, settings and saves come for free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. Scenes are wrapped, so the registered object isn't the live scene. Timers set before the first scene starts are cleared. The font has gaps that show as \"?\". The camera following the cursor made the board slide under the mouse.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hex battlefield and the beast rig.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting hex tactics onto a square-tile renderer.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** A per-letter voice callback in the dialog box, so babble follows the text, and a new module, `engine-ext/beasts.js`, for creature bodies the engine lacked.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** First-class hex maps, plus a documented glyph list for the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The precise deliverables, the freedom to change the engine, and the clear testing rules.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run and assumed far more scope. The wording \"as well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope, and balancing the campaign so it can be won without help.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark plan items as required or stretch, give a scope budget, and have the plan take its run-specific names from TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":565,"cache_read_input_tokens":794630,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":565},"output_tokens":757,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"e4ce581f-56eb-4ffe-81f4-0b39d512db74","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:46:35.321Z","request_id":"req_011CghzPnukubPjQykcmSq9T"}
