{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghzSXoF6uoTbdzGhXz3h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I made no further changes this round. The latest full run, `node tests/run.mjs --browser`, passed 116 of 116, and here is each TASK.md requirement against the test that checks it in the running game.\n\n| TASK.md requirement | Where it is checked |\n|---|---|\n| Single html with the exact name, in the folder root | `t-task` package test; `browser.mjs` loads it from `file://` with no errors |\n| Built on the my-3D2dge handoff | `t-task` engine-inlined test; `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` |\n| Art direction: engine's native style plus new assets | `t-task` builds and animates all 127 unit visuals; screenshots of the title, setup, map, battles, story stage and About |\n| Animation: engine's native system plus new animations | `t-settings` checks that a knight's swing, a wolf's bite, a wyvern's breath, a slime's squash and a pixie's wings move over time |\n| Audio entirely in code, gibberish voices | `t-front` (songs parse, every sound is defined, no audio files); `t-settings` (voices go through the effects bus when On, silent when Off) |\n| Online through AI Hub | AI Hub harness: 2-player, 4-player with latency and loss, host-leave and server scenarios all passed; `t-hub` checks the protocol against a fake hub |\n| Multiplayer on its own | `t-hub`: hot-seat players each get their own Muster and privacy cards, human-vs-human battles, a 4-seat CPU free-for-all |\n| A complete, playable demo | `t-play`: each realm wins through the real scenes, a defeat path ends correctly, and each realm wins on Easy without the test boost; `t-hand`: battles played to the end through input presses only |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset | `t-front` and `t-task`: opened from the pause menu, a slider changes damage and mana, Reset restores the defaults |\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle, any input exits | `t-front` (the full loop reaches every part); `browser.mjs` repeats this in Chromium |\n| Narrative tone like the source | `t-story`: all 61 events play, and every speaker and effect is valid |\n| Controller first, PlayStation glyphs, Xbox option, native keyboard and mouse | `t-front` prompt tests; `t-ui` plays the game through input only; `browser.mjs` checks mouse hover and click in Chromium |\n| No overly dense data screens | Visual check only: the screenshots show at most 8 list rows and stats as bars, and no test measures this |\n| Touch-only: notice, Settings button, attract still runs | `t-front` and `t-task`; `browser.mjs` loads it on an emulated phone |\n| Every setting does something | `t-settings`: each of the 13 settings is switched and its effect observed |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | The build guard plus a scan in `t-task` and `t-data` |\n| Source Information and feedback on the About screen and in the reply | `t-about` checks that the About pages and `exit-interview.json` agree |\n\nOn the remaining points:\n- **Balance changes:** TASK.md sets no stat values, says \"Balance is key\", and overrides the plan. I changed only numbers, never removed a feature.\n- **Hand-played battles:** these are now tested through the controls (`t-hand`). An autoplay bot wins 12 of 20 Easy, 6 of 20 Normal and 0 of 20 Hard campaigns. Those figures use auto-resolved battles; a human commanding battles by hand isn't measured.\n- **The 25 issues and 8 critical ones:** the bugs listed under Testing Feedback, each found and fixed in this session.\n- **Usage:** the session time comes from the system clock. API cost and token counts aren't visible to me, so they're \"unknown\", as TASK.md allows.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:23\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin / Atlus, PlayStation, 1998)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer. The campaign is a three-quarter overhead world map. Battles are a tilted overhead hex board (a custom 50° view, rotatable, with isometric as the alternative). Story scenes are side-on stages.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full build), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, Node 24 for the build and tests; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-style pixel look. Engine character and blob rigs, a new procedural beast rig, hex terrain baked per season, and custom castle and clock-tower shapes.\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal moves and poses, blob squash and stretch, new procedural beast animation, particles, lights and weather.\n- **Audio:** all in code: step-sequencer songs, stings, synthesized sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode, \"Banners of the Wheel\", for 2–4 players with the host's copy as the authority; local hot-seat for 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:** 12 headless Node suites and 7 browser checks behind one command, plus the AI Hub harness (4 of 4 scenarios passed).\n- **Research:** none during the build; the plan records its own.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time by the system clock, 1 h 41 min (05:02:23 to 06:43:44). API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 25, each fixed:\n   - The AI never marched.\n   - The Dominion snowballed.\n   - Autoplay never pushed for Meridian.\n   - The Sanctum could not be won.\n   - EXP was far too slow.\n   - Beaten brigand captains lingered.\n   - A duplicate top-level name broke the bundle.\n   - A story event crashed when its flag was unset.\n   - The tutorial was queued after the Muster.\n   - Dev tools read stale scene state.\n   - Owain joined too early.\n   - Dev mode was blocked during a battle dialog.\n   - The online client sent nothing.\n   - Deep links were dropped.\n   - Missing font glyphs showed as \"?\".\n   - The Sanctum deep link fielded the wrong sides.\n   - The Summon prompt showed a garbled cost.\n   - The Gray Knight looked like a red-haired man.\n   - The camera chased the mouse.\n   - The war could not be won without the boost.\n   - The Dominion fought its own Gray Knight.\n   - The Sanctum was skipped when few knights survived.\n   - The Sanctum reinforcement rule was missing.\n   - Two mistakes in my own test scripts.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 8:\n   - The duplicate name (the game would not load).\n   - The AI never marching.\n   - The unwinnable Sanctum.\n   - No win without the boost.\n   - The Dominion fighting itself (it could end the game with a false loss).\n   - The tutorial out of order.\n   - The silent online client.\n   - The camera chasing the mouse.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The autoplay campaign, especially without the boost.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Testing the dialog box outside the game loop.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The deterministic headless clock and the debug handle. The character rigs, pause menu, settings and saves come free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - Scenes are wrapped, so the registered object isn't the live scene.\n   - Timers set before the first scene starts are cleared.\n   - The font has gaps that show as \"?\".\n   - Making the camera follow the cursor slid the board under the mouse.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hex battlefield and the beast rig.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting hex tactics onto a square-tile renderer.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** A per-letter voice callback in the dialog box, so babble follows the text, and a new module, `engine-ext/beasts.js`, for creature bodies the engine lacked.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** First-class hex maps, plus a documented list of the font's glyphs.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The precise deliverables, the freedom to change the engine, and the clear testing rules.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run and assumed far more scope, and \"as well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope, and balancing the campaign so it can be won without help.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark plan items as required or stretch, give a scope budget, and have the plan take its run-specific names from TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2903,"cache_read_input_tokens":795195,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2903},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"3182d4be-2ffd-4797-ae80-c74cb53a2517","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:47:19.404Z","request_id":"req_011CghzSXKie3xKRTTK9ghkQ"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Deliverables are present (single .html in the folder root, exit-interview.json written and parses, and the final reply has all four sections), but the transcript does not establish that every TASK.md feature is implemented as written. The Sanctum, Gray Knight, Hour-Candle and EXP values were changed from the plan and TASK.md's balance requirement was not checked against them. The 'no overly dense data screens' requirement was verified only visually, with no test. Several per-requirement checks are self-written and shallow (e.g., the animation check builds visuals and plays one attack; the settings and mouse checks were added after ad hoc runs). The 25-issue, 8-critical and usage figures are not tied to logged evidence in the transcript."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"03072eff-be2e-47ec-8b23-878c6b95f80a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:47:34.439Z","isSynthetic":true}
