{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghzha56EPWdiKKBGLLJ5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The final run of `node tests/run.mjs --browser` passed 120 of 120 checks across 13 suites plus the browser checks. The single `.html` is in the folder root, and `exit-interview.json` parses with every field filled and agrees with the About screen.\n\nThe one item that had only a visual check, \"no overly dense data screens,\" is now enforced in code and tested:\n- **8-row cap:** The list widget never shows more than 8 rows, or 5 at the Large UI scale. Dev mode was the one screen that showed 10 rows, and it now uses 8 like everything else.\n- **New `t-density` suite:** It opens the castle panels, title, setup, skirmish, battle and troop menus and every dev tab, and checks that none exceeds the cap. It also confirms stats are drawn as bars with numbers and that there are no HTML tables.\n\nEvery other TASK.md requirement has the test listed in the mapping in my previous reply, and those all still pass. Since then, two suites have been added and dev mode's row limit changed from 10 to 8 to meet the density rule; nothing else changed. The balance changes alter numbers only: the Sanctum, Gray Knight, Season Dials and candle are all present. With every battle auto-resolved, the autoplay campaign wins on Easy and Normal without any stat boost.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:23\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin / Atlus, PlayStation, 1998)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer. The campaign uses a three-quarter overhead world map. Battles use a tilted overhead hex board, a custom 50° view that can rotate, with isometric as an alternative. Story scenes are side-on stages.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full build), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, Node 24 for the build and tests; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-style pixel look. It uses the engine's character and blob rigs plus a new procedural beast rig, hex terrain baked per season, and custom castle and clock-tower shapes.\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal moves and poses, blob squash and stretch, new procedural beast animation, particles, lights and weather.\n- **Audio:** all in code: step-sequencer songs, stings, synthesized sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode, \"Banners of the Wheel,\" for 2–4 players, with the host's copy as the authority. Local hot-seat supports 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:** 13 headless Node suites and 7 browser checks behind one command, plus the AI Hub harness (all 4 scenarios passed).\n- **Research:** none during the build; the plan records its own.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time by the system clock, 1 h 45 min (05:02:23 to 06:48:01). API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 25, each fixed:\n   - The campaign AI never marched.\n   - The Dominion snowballed.\n   - Autoplay never pushed for Meridian.\n   - The Sanctum couldn't be won.\n   - EXP came far too slowly.\n   - Beaten brigand captains lingered.\n   - A duplicate top-level name broke the bundle.\n   - A story event crashed when its flag was unset.\n   - The tutorial was queued after the Muster.\n   - Dev tools read stale scene state.\n   - Owain joined too early.\n   - Dev mode was blocked during a battle dialog.\n   - The online client sent nothing.\n   - Deep links were dropped.\n   - Missing font glyphs showed as \"?\".\n   - The Sanctum deep link put the wrong sides on the field.\n   - The Summon prompt showed the wrong cost.\n   - The Gray Knight looked like a red-haired man.\n   - The camera chased the mouse.\n   - The war couldn't be won without a stat boost.\n   - The Dominion fought its own Gray Knight.\n   - The Sanctum was skipped when few knights survived.\n   - The Sanctum reinforcement rule was missing.\n   - Two mistakes in my own test scripts.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 8:\n   - the duplicate name;\n   - the AI never marching;\n   - the unwinnable Sanctum;\n   - no win without a boost;\n   - the Dominion fighting itself;\n   - the tutorial out of order;\n   - the silent online client;\n   - the camera chasing the mouse.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The autoplay campaign, especially without the boost.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Testing the dialog box outside the game loop.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The deterministic headless clock and debug handle. The character rigs, pause menu, settings and saves come free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - Scenes are wrapped, so the registered object isn't the live scene.\n   - Timers set before the first scene starts are cleared.\n   - The font has gaps that show as \"?\".\n   - The camera following the cursor made the board slide under the mouse.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hex battlefield and the beast rig.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting hex tactics onto a square-tile renderer.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** I added a per-letter voice callback in the dialog box so the babble follows the text. I also added a new module, `engine-ext/beasts.js`, for creature bodies the engine lacked.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** First-class hex maps, plus a documented list of the glyphs the font supports.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The precise deliverables, the freedom to change the engine, and the clear testing rules.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run and assumed far more scope. \"As well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope, and balancing the campaign so it can be won without help.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark plan items as required or stretch, give a scope budget, and have the plan take its run-specific names from TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":289,"cache_read_input_tokens":805773,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":289},"output_tokens":745,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"95a2ac4e-40f1-483f-b5cd-e32751293c56","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:50:35.345Z","request_id":"req_011CghzhZfn8p2XoGmxf2LY8"}
