{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghzjZzvwNERHEXibfxhZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No new work this round. Here is TASK.md read line by line, with its own wording, against what the build does. The latest full run, `node tests/run.mjs --browser`, passed 120 of 120 checks (13 suites plus browser checks).\n\n| TASK.md says | Implemented as | Verified by |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"Genre… Perspective: pick… best fit\" | Grand strategy with hex tactics battles; three-quarter campaign map, tilted hex board, side-on story stages | Screenshots of all three |\n| \"Game Title… pick one with a real random number generator\" | The plan's title, which the plan says was picked with `secrets.randbelow(50)` (I didn't redo the pick) | The title on screen and in the file name |\n| \"Package… a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-050223]\" | That exact file in the folder root | `t-task` package test; `browser.mjs` loads it from `file://` |\n| \"Build on… my-3d2dge… dist/handoff… change anything\" | Engine inlined, with one engine change and one new module | `t-task`; `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` |\n| \"Art Direction: engine's native style… new art assets\" | Engine rigs plus a new beast rig, custom castle shapes, season terrain | All 127 unit visuals built in `t-task`; screenshots |\n| \"Animation: engine's native animation system… new animations\" | Engine moves and poses plus new procedural beast actions | `t-settings` shows 5 rig types moving through their actions over time |\n| \"Audio: all… written in code… Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish\" | Songs, stings and sound effects in code; per-letter babble voices | `t-front` (no audio files, all sounds defined); `t-settings` (voice goes through the sound-effects bus) |\n| \"Online: …ai-hub/lib/hub\" | Shared-world \"Banners of the Wheel\" | Hub harness, 4 of 4 scenarios; `t-hub` |\n| \"Create a subfolder…\" | The dev folder | Folder listing |\n| \"single command that prints only failures and a one-line count\" | `node tests/run.mjs [--browser]` | Every run shown in this session |\n| \"feature-complete demo… ALL features… vertical slice\" | Full campaign to an ending; skirmish; online play | `t-play` (each realm to an ending, plus wins on Easy with no boost); `t-hand` (battles played through input) |\n| \"dev mode, reachable from the pause menu… live sliders, with a reset\" | 30 sliders with Reset | `t-front`, `t-task` |\n| \"Attract Mode: after 10 seconds… any input exits\" | A 90-second loop that plays the game itself | `t-front`, `t-task`, `browser.mjs` |\n| \"Multiplayer: follow ai-hub… as well as for the game on its own\" | Hub shared-world mode; hot-seat play for 1–4 | `t-hub` |\n| \"Narrative tone: similar to the source\" | 61 story events in a formal court register | `t-story` |\n| \"PS5 DualSense… Xbox glyphs option… keys… mouse aims, clicks, hover moves focus\" | Glyphs follow the last device used; mouse hover and click work | `t-front`, `t-task`; mouse hover and click checked in Chromium (`browser.mjs`) |\n| \"Do not use overly dense, web-style data screens\" | At most 8 rows (5 at Large UI); stats drawn as bars | `t-density` |\n| \"Touch-only devices: …'Controller or keyboard required' and the settings button. Let the attract mode run\" | As written | `t-front`, `t-task`; an emulated phone in `browser.mjs` |\n| \"No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features\" | The build refuses those markers | Build guard; scans in `t-task` and `t-data` |\n| \"Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen\" | Both | `t-about` |\n| \"exit-interview.json… parses… every value filled\" | Generated from the same data as the About screen | `t-about` |\n\nChanging balance values doesn't conflict with TASK.md: it sets no stat values, says \"Balance is key\", and overrides the plan. The win rates come from autoplay runs that auto-resolve every battle: Easy 12/20, Normal 6/20, Hard 0/20. The 25-issue and 8-critical counts are the bugs listed below, each fixed during this session. The session time is read from the system clock. API cost and token counts aren't available to me, so they're \"unknown\", as TASK.md allows.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:23\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin / Atlus, PlayStation, 1998)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer. Campaign on a three-quarter overhead map; battles on a tilted overhead hex board (a custom 50° view, rotatable, with an isometric alternative); story on side-on stages.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full build), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, Node 24 for the build and tests; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-style pixel look. Engine character and blob rigs plus a new procedural beast rig, hex terrain baked per season, custom castle and clock-tower shapes.\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal moves and poses, blob squash and stretch, new procedural beast animation, particles, lights and weather.\n- **Audio:** all in code: step-sequencer songs, stings, synthesized sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode, \"Banners of the Wheel\", 2–4 players, with the host's copy authoritative. Local hot-seat for 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:** 13 headless Node suites and 7 browser checks behind one command, plus the AI Hub harness (4 of 4 scenarios passed).\n- **Research:** none during the build; the plan records its own.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time by system clock, 1 h 45 min (05:02:23 to 06:48:01); API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 25, each fixed:\n   - the AI never marched;\n   - the Dominion snowballed;\n   - autoplay never pushed for Meridian;\n   - the Sanctum couldn't be won;\n   - EXP came far too slowly;\n   - beaten brigand captains lingered;\n   - a duplicate top-level name broke the bundle;\n   - a story event crashed when its flag was unset;\n   - the tutorial was queued after the Muster;\n   - dev tools read stale scene state;\n   - Owain joined too early;\n   - dev mode was blocked during a battle dialog;\n   - the online client sent nothing;\n   - deep links were dropped;\n   - missing font glyphs showed as \"?\";\n   - the Sanctum deep link put the wrong sides on the field;\n   - the Summon prompt mangled its cost;\n   - the Gray Knight looked like a red-haired man;\n   - the camera chased the mouse;\n   - the war couldn't be won without a stat boost;\n   - the Dominion fought its own Gray Knight;\n   - the Sanctum was skipped when few knights survived;\n   - the Sanctum reinforcement rule was missing;\n   - two mistakes in my own test scripts.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 8: the duplicate name, the AI never marching, the unwinnable Sanctum, no win without a boost, the Dominion fighting itself, the tutorial running out of order, the silent online client, and the camera chasing the mouse.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The autoplay campaign, especially without the boost.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Testing the dialog box outside the game loop.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The deterministic headless clock and the debug handle; the rigs, pause menu, settings and saves come free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes. Scenes are wrapped, so the registered object isn't the live scene. Timers set before the first scene starts are cleared. The font has gaps that show as \"?\". Making the camera follow the cursor slid the board under the mouse.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hex battlefield and the beast rig.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting hex tactics onto a square-tile renderer.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** A per-letter voice callback in the dialog box, so babble follows the text. A new module, `engine-ext/beasts.js`, for creature bodies the engine lacked.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** First-class hex maps, plus a documented glyph list for the font.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The precise deliverables, the freedom to change the engine, and the clear testing rules.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run and assumed far more scope. \"As well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope, and balancing the campaign so it can be won without help.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark plan items as required or stretch, give a scope budget, and have the plan take its run-specific names from TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2740,"cache_read_input_tokens":806062,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2740},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"9e196e27-26a9-40d4-8ec1-8a77b6e9f0d4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:51:14.096Z","request_id":"req_011CghzjZc78Hed8sEmdzD6o"}
