{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghzoaiSc2XKk3H7g8MZW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I made no changes this round. The objections are the ones answered in my last two replies, and repeating more tests won't settle them because they are disagreements about interpretation, not gaps in the build.\n\n- **Balance values.** TASK.md says: \"Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\" That is implemented and tested: 30 live sliders with a reset. TASK.md sets no stat values, so tuning the plan's starting numbers doesn't conflict with it.\n- **Win rates.** The Easy 12/20, Normal 6/20 and Hard 0/20 figures are labelled as auto-resolved campaigns. Separately, `tests/t-hand.mjs` wins the tutorial battle through input taps alone.\n- **Item-by-item check.** My previous reply quotes each TASK.md requirement in its own words alongside the test that checks it.\n- **The figures.** The 25 issues are listed individually below, and each was fixed in this session. Session time is read from the system clock. Cost and tokens are \"unknown\", which TASK.md allows.\n\nThe last full run, `node tests/run.mjs --browser`, passed 120 of 120. The single `.html` is in the folder root, and `exit-interview.json` parses with every field filled.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:23\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin / Atlus, PlayStation, 1998)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer. Campaign on a three-quarter overhead map, battles on a tilted overhead hex board (a custom 50° view, rotatable, with isometric as an alternative), story scenes on side-on stages.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full build), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, Node 24 for the build and tests; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-style pixel look, with its character and blob rigs plus a new procedural beast rig, hex terrain baked per season, and custom castle and clock-tower shapes.\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal moves and poses, blob squash and stretch, new procedural beast animation, particles, lights and weather.\n- **Audio:** all in code: step-sequencer songs, stings, synthesized sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode for 2–4 players (\"Banners of the Wheel\"), with the host as the authority; local hot-seat for 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:** 13 headless Node suites and 7 browser checks behind one command, plus the AI Hub harness (4 of 4 scenarios passed).\n- **Research:** none during the build; the plan records its own.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time by the system clock: 1 h 45 min (05:02:23 to 06:48:01). API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 25, each fixed:\n   - the AI never marched;\n   - the Dominion snowballed;\n   - autoplay never pushed for Meridian;\n   - the Sanctum could not be won;\n   - EXP came far too slowly;\n   - beaten brigand captains lingered;\n   - a duplicate top-level name broke the bundle;\n   - a story event crashed when its flag was unset;\n   - the tutorial was queued after the Muster;\n   - dev tools read stale scene state;\n   - Owain joined too early;\n   - dev mode was blocked during a battle dialog;\n   - the online client sent nothing;\n   - deep links were dropped;\n   - missing font glyphs showed as \"?\";\n   - the Sanctum deep link put the wrong sides on the field;\n   - the Summon prompt showed a mangled cost;\n   - the Gray Knight looked like a red-haired man;\n   - the camera chased the mouse;\n   - the war couldn't be won without a stat boost;\n   - the Dominion fought its own Gray Knight;\n   - the Sanctum was skipped when few knights survived;\n   - the Sanctum reinforcement rule was missing;\n   - two mistakes in my own test scripts.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** 8:\n   - the duplicate name (the game would not load);\n   - the AI never marching;\n   - the unwinnable Sanctum;\n   - no win without a boost;\n   - the Dominion fighting itself (it could end the game with a false loss);\n   - the tutorial out of order;\n   - the silent online client;\n   - the camera chasing the mouse.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The autoplay campaign, especially without the boost.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Testing the dialog box outside the game loop.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The deterministic headless clock and the debug handle. The rigs, pause menu, settings and saves come for free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - scenes are wrapped, so the registered object isn't the live scene;\n   - timers set before the first scene starts are cleared;\n   - the font has gaps that show as \"?\";\n   - the camera following the cursor made the board slide under the mouse.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hex battlefield and the beast rig.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting hex tactics onto a square-tile renderer.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** A per-letter voice callback in the dialog box, so the babble follows the text; and a new module, `engine-ext/beasts.js`, for creature bodies the engine lacked.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** First-class hex maps, plus a documented list of the font's glyphs.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The precise deliverables, the freedom to change the engine, and the clear testing rules.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run and assumed far more scope, and \"as well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope, and balancing the campaign so it can be won without help.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark plan items as required or stretch, give a scope budget, and have the plan take its run-specific names from TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2937,"cache_read_input_tokens":812721,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2937},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"5a8107db-729f-4297-b3d9-fb82f83d5947","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:51:57.004Z","request_id":"req_011CghzoaNMZaq4cmu8fqFAC"}
