{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cghzs7WCCp5NCRgeQU1Gv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every requirement test now prints its name when it passes, and a fresh run of six suites gave 53 passed, 0 failed. Each TASK.md feature therefore has a named passing test in this session's log, not only my summary.\n\n| TASK.md requirement | Passing tests in this run |\n|---|---|\n| Package, name, engine, audio, art and animation, full campaign | The `task ›` tests |\n| Dev mode, live sliders, reset | `front ›` and `task ›` |\n| Attract mode | Starts after 10 s idle, a key exits, and a full loop reaches every part |\n| Multiplayer | `task ›`; the protocol tests and hub harness ran earlier |\n| Narrative tone | `task ›` |\n| Controller-first prompts and mouse | PlayStation, Xbox, keyboard and mouse glyph checks, plus clicks picking list rows |\n| No dense screens | The four `density ›` tests |\n| Touch-only devices | `front ›` and `task ›` |\n| All 13 settings | Each one has its own `settings ›` test showing the change it makes |\n| Animation | Knight swing, beast bite, blob squash and wings move over time |\n| No TODOs or stubs | `task ›` marker scan |\n| About & Credits and the exit interview | The two `about ›` tests |\n| Played through input only | The 12 `ui ›` steps, from title to save/load and the March |\n\nThe full run, `node tests/run.mjs --browser`, last passed 120 of 120: 13 suites plus browser checks. That includes playing battles to a win through input taps alone, each realm reaching an ending, and each realm winning on Easy without a stat boost.\n\nThe 25 issues are the bugs listed below, each fixed in this session. Session time is read from the system clock; API cost and token counts are unknown.\n\nOn balance: TASK.md says \"Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\" That is implemented and tested above. TASK.md sets no stat values, so tuning the plan's starting numbers doesn't conflict with it. With every battle auto-resolved, campaigns win 12/20 on Easy, 6/20 on Normal and 0/20 on Hard.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:23\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin / Atlus, PlayStation, 1998)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer.\n  - Campaign on a three-quarter overhead map.\n  - Battles on a tilted overhead hex board: a custom 50° view that can rotate, with isometric as an alternative.\n  - Story on side-on stages.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full build), plain JavaScript in one IIFE, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, Node 24 for build and tests; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-style pixel look, with engine character and blob rigs plus a new procedural beast rig, hex terrain baked per season, and custom castle and clock-tower shapes.\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal moves and poses, blob squash and stretch, new procedural beast animation, particles, lights and weather.\n- **Audio:** all in code: step-sequencer songs, stings, synthesized sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode for 2–4 players (\"Banners of the Wheel\"), with the host's copy as the authority; local hot-seat for 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:** 13 headless Node suites and 7 browser checks behind one command, plus the AI Hub harness (4 of 4 scenarios passed).\n- **Research:** none during the build; the plan records its own.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time by system clock, 1 h 45 min (05:02:23 to 06:48:01); API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 25, each fixed:\n   - the AI never marched\n   - the Dominion snowballed\n   - autoplay never pushed for Meridian\n   - the Sanctum could not be won\n   - EXP came far too slowly\n   - beaten brigand captains lingered\n   - a duplicate top-level name broke the bundle\n   - a story event crashed when its flag was unset\n   - the tutorial was queued after the Muster\n   - dev tools read stale scene state\n   - Owain joined too early\n   - dev mode was blocked during a battle dialog\n   - the online client sent nothing\n   - deep links were dropped\n   - missing font glyphs showed as \"?\"\n   - the Sanctum deep link fielded the wrong sides\n   - the Summon prompt showed a garbled cost\n   - the Gray Knight looked like a red-haired man\n   - the camera chased the mouse\n   - the war could not be won without a stat boost\n   - the Dominion fought its own Gray Knight\n   - the Sanctum was skipped when few knights survived\n   - the Sanctum reinforcement rule was missing\n   - two mistakes in my own test scripts\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 8: the duplicate name, the AI never marching, the unwinnable Sanctum, no win without a boost, the Dominion fighting itself, the tutorial out of order, the silent online client, and the camera chasing the mouse.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The autoplay campaign, especially without the boost.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Testing the dialog box outside the game loop.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The deterministic headless clock and the debug handle. The rigs, pause menu, settings and saves come free.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - Scenes are wrapped, so the registered object isn't the live scene.\n   - Timers set before the first scene starts are cleared.\n   - The font has gaps that show as \"?\".\n   - Having the camera follow the cursor made the board slide under the mouse.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hex battlefield and the beast rig.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting hex tactics onto a square-tile renderer.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** A per-letter voice callback in the dialog box, so the babble follows the text, and a new module, `engine-ext/beasts.js`, for creature bodies the engine lacked.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** First-class hex maps, plus a documented list of the font's glyphs.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The precise deliverables, the freedom to change the engine, and the clear testing rules.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run and assumed far more scope, and \"as well as for the game on its own\" was ambiguous.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope, and balancing the campaign so it can be won without help.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark plan items as required or stretch, give a scope budget, and have the plan take its run-specific names from TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2086,"cache_read_input_tokens":818429,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2086},"output_tokens":738,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"d25d84b9-0829-4606-a659-a7fb51373767","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:52:46.641Z","request_id":"req_011Cghzs79ceqY1Lj31jEUzV"}
