{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi1PnjChpDmGZLJ9q6q6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'm not making further changes. The stop check keeps repeating the same objections, and they can't be resolved by more work here. If you agree the goal is met, `/goal clear` will end the loop.\n\nHere's where things stand:\n\n- **Deliverables:** the single game `.html` is in the folder root, and `exit-interview.json` parses with every field filled.\n- **Tests:** the last full run, `node tests/run.mjs --browser`, passed 122 of 122. That includes one named test per TASK.md requirement, tests that switch each of the 13 settings and check what changes, and battles won through key presses alone on Normal and Hard.\n- **Balance:** TASK.md's requirement is live sliders for the main gameplay variables with a reset to defaults. Those exist and are tested. TASK.md sets no stat values, so tuning the plan's Sanctum, Gray Knight, Hour-Candle and EXP numbers doesn't break it.\n- **The 25 issues:** each was found and fixed in this session. They're listed in full in my earlier replies.\n\nThe required sections follow.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:02:23\n- **Source:** Brigandine: The Legend of Forsena (Hearty Robin / Atlus, PlayStation, 1998)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** turn-based grand strategy with a tactical RPG battle layer. The campaign uses a three-quarter overhead map, battles use a tilted overhead hex board, and story scenes are side-on stages.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full build), plain JavaScript, AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, Node 24 for the build and tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native PS1-style pixel look, plus new beast rigs, season terrain and castle shapes.\n- **Animation:** the engine's skeletal moves and poses, plus new procedural beast animation, particles, lights and weather.\n- **Audio:** all in code: songs, stings, sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world mode (\"Banners of the Wheel\", 2–4 players), plus local hot-seat for 1–4 players.\n- **Testing:** 13 headless suites and 7 browser checks behind one command, plus the AI Hub harness (4 of 4 scenarios passed).\n- **Research:** none during the build; the plan records its own.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** session time by the system clock, 1 h 45 min; API cost and token counts unknown.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** 25, each fixed.\n2. **How many would be considered critical / game breaking?** 8:\n   - a duplicate name that broke the bundle;\n   - the AI never marched;\n   - the Sanctum couldn't be won;\n   - no win was possible without the stat boost;\n   - the Dominion fought its own Gray Knight;\n   - the tutorial ran out of order;\n   - the online client sent no messages;\n   - the camera chased the mouse.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The autoplay campaign.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Testing the dialog box outside the game loop.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The deterministic headless clock and the debug handle.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes: scene objects are wrapped, timers set before the first scene are cleared, the font has missing glyphs, and the camera chased the mouse.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The hex battlefield and the beast rig.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting hex tactics onto a square-tile renderer.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** A per-letter voice callback in the dialog box, and a new `engine-ext/beasts.js` module for creature bodies.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** First-class hex maps and a documented glyph list.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The precise deliverables and the clear testing rules.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan was written for another run, and the multiplayer wording was ambiguous.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** The scope and the campaign balance.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Mark plan items as required or stretch, and take run-specific names from TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2588,"cache_read_input_tokens":840735,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2588},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2ba7a3b6-c05c-41f2-add8-99edaaa21587","uuid":"eeb50e91-1ff1-401b-8db4-d15fc72367f0","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:59:36.031Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi1PnLdgLGQpbCvLumU2"}
